OAI_PAPER({"slug": "The-Selmer-converse-for-elliptic-curves-at-every-prime-October-7-2026", "title": "The Selmer converse for elliptic curves at every prime", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Selmer-converse-for-elliptic-curves-at-every-prime-October-7-2026/main.pdf", "family": "002", "family_title": "The full BSD formula from low Selmer corank", "abstract_html": "We prove the Selmer converse in coranks zero and one for every elliptic curve over ℚ and every prime <i>p</i>: if the full <i>p</i>-power Selmer group has ℤ<sub><i>p</i></sub>-corank \\(r\\in\\{0,1\\}\\), then the analytic and Mordell–Weil ranks both equal <i>r</i>, and the entire Tate–Shafarevich group is finite. As an application at the additive prime 3, we prove that for every prime \\(\\ell\\equiv4,7,8\\pmod9\\), the cubic \\(X^3+Y^3=\\ell Z^3\\) has analytic and Mordell–Weil rank one and finite Tate–Shafarevich group. In particular, every such <i>ℓ</i> is a sum of two rational cubes.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1, “The unrestricted low-corank Selmer converse.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A_0/\\mathbf Q\\) be an elliptic curve and \\(p\\) a rational prime.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Sha(A_0/\\mathbf Q)\\) be the kernel of localization from the Weil–Châtelet group to the product of the local Weil–Châtelet groups.</p><p class=\"li\">The full \\(p\\)-power Selmer group has the local Kummer condition at every place and fits into\n  \\[\n  0\\longrightarrow A_0(\\mathbf Q)\\otimes \\mathbf Q_p/\\mathbf Z_p\n  \\longrightarrow \\operatorname{Sel}_{p^\\infty}(A_0/\\mathbf Q)\n  \\longrightarrow \\Sha(A_0/\\mathbf Q)[p^\\infty]\n  \\longrightarrow 0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  s_p(A_0)=\\operatorname{corank}_{\\mathbf Z_p}\n  \\operatorname{Sel}_{p^\\infty}(A_0/\\mathbf Q),\n  \\qquad\n  a(A_0)=\\operatorname{ord}_{s=1}L(A_0,s).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(r\\in\\{0,1\\}\\),\n\\[\ns_p(A_0)=r\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\na(A_0)=\\operatorname{rank}_{\\mathbf Z}A_0(\\mathbf Q)=r,\n\\qquad\n\\#\\Sha(A_0/\\mathbf Q)&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nThere is no restriction on reduction type, complex multiplication, the residual representation, rational torsion, or rational isogenies. The assertion concerns the entire Tate–Shafarevich group; it does not evaluate the leading coefficient in the refined Birch–Swinnerton-Dyer formula.</p>", "proof": "<p>The external converse at \\(2\\), stated in Proposition 1 as the paper’s Theorem 2.1, proves the result when \\(p=2\\).</p><p>Let \\(p&gt;2\\). Proposition 1 selects \\(K,F,L\\) and reduces the desired conclusion to non-torsion of the conductor-one Heegner trace \\(y\\). Assume \\(y\\) torsion.</p><p>Proposition 2 constructs the tame deformation and proves\n\\[\n\\operatorname{length}_{R_{b_*}}\nH_{\\mathrm{Gr}}^1(L,V\\otimes\\psi^{\\pm1})&lt;b_*.\n\\]\nProposition 3 gives the corresponding weighted Heegner-logarithm vanishing.</p><p>Propositions 4–7 construct integral holomorphic theta forms whose constant terms tend to zero in the full truncated tame ring, while a positive Fourier–Jacobi coefficient has bounded augmentation divisibility. Proposition 8 lifts them to genuine cusp forms and factors their comparison values through the entire operator order. Propositions 9–11 attach simultaneous characteristic-zero Galois representations and retain the needed local flags and inertia identities. Proposition 12 removes operator nilpotents with only fixed precision loss.</p><p>Propositions 13–15 construct the finite limiting algebra, eliminate the unwanted cyclotomic corner, and show that the desired corner has zero zeroth Fitting ideal. Proposition 16 then gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{length}_{R_{b_*}}\nH_{\\mathrm{Gr}}^1(L,V\\otimes\\psi^{\\pm1})\\ge b_*.\n\\]\nThis contradicts Proposition 2: outside the strict case the bounds are \\(0&lt;1\\); in the strict case they are at most \\(2&lt;3\\).</p><p>Thus \\(y\\) is non-torsion. Proposition 1, Gross–Zagier, and Kolyvagin give\n\\[\na(A_0)=\\operatorname{rank}A_0(\\mathbf Q)=r\n\\]\nand finiteness of the entire Tate–Shafarevich group.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Corollary 10.1, “Sylvester’s positive prime cases.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\ell\\) be a rational prime with\n  \\[\n  \\ell\\equiv 4,7,8\\pmod 9.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Equip\n  \\[\n  E_\\ell:\\ X^3+Y^3=\\ell Z^3\n  \\]\n  with origin \\((1:-1:0)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\operatorname{ord}_{s=1}L(E_\\ell,s)\n=\n\\operatorname{rank}_{\\mathbf Z}E_\\ell(\\mathbf Q)\n=1,\n\\qquad\n\\#\\Sha(E_\\ell/\\mathbf Q)&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nIn particular, there exist \\(x,y\\in\\mathbf Q\\) such that\n\\[\nx^3+y^3=\\ell.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 17 gives \\(s_3(E_\\ell)\\in\\{0,1\\}\\). Apply Theorem 1 at the additive prime \\(3\\). It gives equality of analytic rank, Mordell–Weil rank, and this corank, and finiteness of the whole \\(\\Sha\\).</p><p>For \\(\\ell\\equiv4,7,8\\pmod9\\), the functional-equation sign is \\(-1\\), as cited from Dasgupta–Voight. Therefore \\(L(E_\\ell,1)=0\\), excluding analytic rank zero. The common rank is one.</p><p>A non-torsion rational point on the projective cubic is not its origin. The origin is the only rational point with \\(Z=0\\), so the point has \\(Z\\ne0\\). Dividing its equation by \\(Z^3\\) gives a rational cube-sum representation.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "Lemma A.1, “Comparison of global torus factors.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K=\\mathbf F_q(X)\\), where \\(X\\) is a smooth projective geometrically connected curve of genus \\(g\\), and let \\(\\Gamma_K\\) be its absolute Galois group.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(T_1,T_2\\) be \\(K\\)-tori whose rational character modules are isomorphic as Galois representations.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  \\ell:\\operatorname{Lie}T_1\\xrightarrow{\\sim}\\operatorname{Lie}T_2\n  \\]\n  be a \\(K\\)-linear isomorphism.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose local Haar measures \\(\\eta_{i,v}\\) compatible under \\(\\ell\\) on the Lie algebras, giving the smooth integral compact subgroups volume one at almost all places. Write \\(\\eta_i\\) for the resulting adelic measures and use counting measure on \\(T_i(K)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a \\(K\\)-torus \\(T\\), put \\(Y=X^*(T)\\), \\(r=\\operatorname{rank}Y^{\\Gamma_K}\\), and\n  \\[\n  \\deg_T(t)(\\chi)\n  =\n  \\sum_v[k(v):\\mathbf F_q]\\,v(\\chi(t_v)),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  I(T)=\n  \\bigl[\n  \\operatorname{Hom}(Y^{\\Gamma_K},\\mathbf Z):\n  \\deg_T(T(\\mathbf A_K))\n  \\bigr].\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  T(\\mathbf A_K)^1=\\ker\\deg_T,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\Sha^1(K,T)\n  =\n  \\ker\\left(H^1(K,T)\\longrightarrow\\prod_vH^1(K_v,T)\\right),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  b(T)=\\left|\\pi_0(\\widehat T^{\\Gamma_K})\\right|,\n  \\qquad\n  c_\\eta(T)\n  =\n  \\operatorname{vol}_\\eta\\bigl(T(K)\\backslash T(\\mathbf A_K)^1\\bigr)\n  |\\Sha^1(K,T)|.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\frac{c_{\\eta_1}(T_1)/b(T_1)}\n     {c_{\\eta_2}(T_2)/b(T_2)}\n=\n\\frac{I(T_1)}{I(T_2)}.\n\\]\nIn particular, the two normalized factors agree if each torus splits at a place of degree one. The places may be different.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(Y=X^*(T)\\). The dual-torus exponential sequence is\n\\[\n0\\longrightarrow Y\\longrightarrow Y\\otimes_{\\mathbf Z}\\mathbf C\n\\longrightarrow\\widehat T(\\mathbf C)\\longrightarrow1.\n\\]\nThe Galois action factors through a finite group. Averaging on the vector space identifies the component group of \\(\\widehat T^{\\Gamma_K}\\) with \\(H^1(K,Y)\\), since the invariant vector-space image is its identity component. Hence\n\\[\nb(T)=|H^1(K,Y)|.\n\\]</p><p>Choose invariant top forms \\(\\omega_i\\) with \\(\\ell^*\\omega_2=\\omega_1\\). Compatibility means\n\\[\n\\eta_{i,v}=a_v|\\omega_i|_v\n\\]\nwith the same \\(a_v&gt;0\\). Let \\(d=\\dim T_i\\), let \\(P_v(T,t)\\) be the Artin polynomial, and put\n\\[\n\\rho(T)=\\lim_{s\\to1}(s-1)^rL(T,s).\n\\]\nThe rational character-module isomorphism gives identical \\(d,r,P_v,\\rho\\) for the two tori.</p><p>In the Geisser–Suzuki conventions,\n\\[\n\\mu_i=\n\\frac1{\\rho q^{(g-1)d}}\n\\prod_vP_v(q_v^{-1})^{-1}|\\omega_i|_v.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n\\eta_i=\\alpha\\mu_i,\n\\qquad\n\\alpha=\\rho q^{(g-1)d}\\prod_va_vP_v(q_v^{-1}),\n\\]\nwith the same \\(\\alpha\\). At almost all places the forms are integral and the compact volume under \\(|\\omega_i|_v\\) is \\(P_v(q_v^{-1})\\), so only finitely many product factors differ from one. Scaling a global form by \\(K^\\times\\) changes no global measure by the product formula.</p><p>The function-field Tamagawa formula, including the degree correction, is\n\\[\n\\operatorname{vol}_{\\mu_i}\n(T_i(K)\\backslash T_i(\\mathbf A_K)^1)\n=\n(\\log q)^rI(T_i)\\tau(T_i),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\tau(T_i)\n=\n\\frac{|H^1(K,X^*(T_i))|}\n     {|\\Sha^1(K,T_i)|}.\n\\]\nThe discriminant of the degree pairing is exactly \\(I(T_i)\\). Consequently\n\\[\n\\frac{c_{\\eta_i}(T_i)}{b(T_i)}\n=\n\\alpha(\\log q)^rI(T_i).\n\\]\nTaking the ratio proves the theorem.</p><p>If \\(T\\) splits at a degree-one place \\(v_0\\), the invariant lattice \\(Y^{\\Gamma_K}\\) is saturated in \\(Y\\). Restriction therefore surjects\n\\[\nX_*(T_{K_{v_0}})\n=\n\\operatorname{Hom}(Y,\\mathbf Z)\n\\longrightarrow\n\\operatorname{Hom}(Y^{\\Gamma_K},\\mathbf Z).\n\\]\nFor a local cocharacter \\(\\lambda\\), the adele \\(\\lambda(\\varpi_{v_0})\\), equal to one elsewhere, has degree \\(\\chi\\mapsto\\langle\\chi,\\lambda\\rangle\\). Hence \\(I(T)=1\\).</p><p>The paper applies this calculation to the global nonstandard comparison as follows. For the generic regular centralizers \\(T_i\\) of Halleck-Dubé’s construction, the cameral-equivariant rational root-ray correspondence gives isomorphic rational character modules and, after clearing denominators, an isogeny. Its invertible differential gives the prescribed Lie-algebra measure comparison. The marked point is rational and makes both tori split there, so both degree indices are one.</p><p>Chaudouard–Laumon identify the relevant Frobenius-coinvariant kernel with \\(\\Sha^1(F_0,T_i)\\). This is the infinite-cyclic Weil-group statement, not a silent replacement by continuous Galois cohomology. The rational marked-point section acts trivially on the character lattice, so arithmetic and geometric monodromy images coincide. Therefore the exact centralizer cancellation is\n\\[\n\\frac{c(J_{1,a_M})}{|\\pi_0(\\widehat T_1^{W_a})|}\n=\n\\frac{c(J_{2,a_M})}{|\\pi_0(\\widehat T_2^{W_a})|}.\n\\]\nThe separate numerators and denominators need not coincide.</p><p>For the remaining coroot covolumes, let \\(S_i\\) be pinned maximal tori, \\(\\Gamma\\) the root-datum monodromy, and\n\\[\n\\Lambda_{H_i}=\\mathbf Z\\langle\\Phi_{H_i}^\\vee\\rangle\n\\quad(H_i=M_i,G_i).\n\\]\nThe rational map \\(\\psi_*\\) identifies the corresponding rational coinvariant spaces. Put\n\\[\nX_*(H_i)=((X_*(S_i)/\\Lambda_{H_i})_\\Gamma)_{\\mathrm{tf}},\n\\qquad\n\\mathfrak a_{H_i}=X_*(H_i)\\otimes\\mathbf R,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nV_{H_i}\n=\n\\operatorname{vol}\n\\bigl(\\mathfrak a_{S_i}^{H_i}/(\\Lambda_{H_i})_\\Gamma\\bigr).\n\\]\nWhen \\(\\mathfrak a_{G_i}=0\\), the covolume formula gives\n\\[\n\\frac{V_{G_i}}{V_{M_i}}\n=\n\\frac{|\\pi_0(Z_{\\widehat G_i}^{\\Gamma})|}\n     {|\\pi_0(Z_{\\widehat M_i}^{\\Gamma})|}\n\\operatorname{vol}(\\mathfrak a_{M_i}/X_*(M_i)).\n\\]\nWithin each group the torus covolume and component factor cancel. Transporting measures gives\n\\[\n\\frac{V_{H_1}}{V_{H_2}}\n=\n[(\\Lambda_{H_2})_\\Gamma:\n\\psi_*((\\Lambda_{H_1})_\\Gamma)].\n\\]\nThus the residual coefficient is\n\\[\nc_\\psi\n=\n\\frac{V_{G_1}V_{M_2}}{V_{G_2}V_{M_1}}\n=\n\\frac{\n[(\\Lambda_{G_2})_\\Gamma:\\psi_*((\\Lambda_{G_1})_\\Gamma)]\n}{\n[(\\Lambda_{M_2})_\\Gamma:\\psi_*((\\Lambda_{M_1})_\\Gamma)]\n}.\n\\]\nSimple coroots are permutation bases, and \\(\\psi_*\\) scales each orbit by a common scalar, so invariant and coinvariant relative indices agree. Quotienting maximal split centers gives the general case and preserves the weighted integrals, recursion, and coefficient.</p><p>This supplies the normalized global comparison without asserting an isomorphism of generic centralizer tori. The geometric Picard isogeny, cohomological comparison, and support results remain the cited external inputs.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 4", "orig": "Lemma A.2, “Change of characteristic for fixed nonstandard data.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a finite collection of paired based root data with rational root-ray maps \\(\\psi\\), matching Levi data, and compatible finite cyclic outer actions.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose the nonstandard weighted fundamental lemma holds over local fields of sufficiently large positive characteristic for their unramified forms, including all cyclic-generator variants.</p><p class=\"li\">Normalize ambient hyperspecial subgroups to have volume one.</p><p class=\"li\">Transport Arthur weight-space measures through \\(\\psi\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Transport paired regular-centralizer measures through the induced Lie-algebra isomorphism.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The nonstandard weighted fundamental lemma holds over characteristic-zero local fields of sufficiently large residue characteristic, uniformly for the fixed collection and for every integral generically regular matching parameter.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply the Cluckers–Hales–Loeser transfer principle for parameterized integrals by expressing the desired identity as vanishing of one constructible function.</p><p>Choose \\(N\\) excluding all bad primes for the finite root-data collection, Chevalley and Kostant constructions, and outer actions, and making \\(\\psi,\\psi^{-1}\\) integral over \\(\\mathbf Z[1/N]\\). Include the finitely many ordinary endoscopic descendants. The Weyl-equivariant Cartan maps give common bases\n\\[\nC_M=\\mathfrak t_1/W_{M_1}\\simeq\\mathfrak t_2/W_{M_2},\n\\qquad\nC_G=\\mathfrak t_1/W_{G_1}\\simeq\\mathfrak t_2/W_{G_2},\n\\]\ncompatible with \\(C_M\\to C_G\\), integral models, regular loci, and outer actions. These identifications persist after common unramified twisting.</p><p>For an integral generically \\(G\\)-regular \\(b\\), let \\(Y_{i,b}\\) be its Kostant lift in \\(\\operatorname{Lie}(M_i)\\). The identity is\n\\[\nS_{M_1}^{G_1}(b)=c_\\psi S_{M_2}^{G_2}(b).\n\\]</p><p>Encode an unramified splitting extension of fixed degree \\(d\\) by a monic integral polynomial with irreducible separable reduction and the matrix of an algebra automorphism \\(\\tau\\) of exact order \\(d\\). These are fixed-degree first-order conditions and parameterize every required extension. Use the same extension and generator on both sides. The common definable space consists of\n\\[\n(a,\\tau,b),\\qquad\nb\\in C_{M,a,\\tau}(\\mathcal O_F)\n\\]\ngenerically \\(G\\)-regular, where the descended model is given by common semilinear fixed-point equations. The map \\(\\psi\\) intertwines them. Varying \\(\\tau\\) supplies exactly the stipulated cyclic-generator variants.</p><p>On the regular locus, the centralizers \\(J_i\\) are twists of pinned tori along the same cameral torsor. Clearing a denominator in \\(\\psi\\) gives a torus isogeny; its differential, divided by that denominator, gives\n\\[\n\\lambda:\\operatorname{Lie}J_1\\xrightarrow{\\sim}\\operatorname{Lie}J_2.\n\\]\nEnlarge parameters by a nonzero top covector \\(\\omega_1\\) and put\n\\[\n\\omega_2=(\\lambda^{-1})^*\\omega_1.\n\\]\nThese parameterize compatible centralizer measures; common rescaling affects both sides equally.</p><p>For each ambient group \\(H\\) in the finite recursions, add an invariant top-form frame \\(\\eta_H\\). The exact sequence\n\\[\n0\\to\\operatorname{Lie}J_b\\to\\operatorname{Lie}H\n\\xrightarrow{Z\\mapsto[Z,Y]}\nT_YX_b\\to0\n\\]\ndefines the quotient orbital form. On smooth charts, comparison with motivic measure is an absolute rational determinant, hence a constructible valuation factor. Let\n\\[\nV_H=\\int_{K_H}|\\eta_H|&gt;0.\n\\]\nThis is constructible, and normalized ambient measure is \\(|\\eta_H|/V_H\\). Clear these finitely many volume factors later.</p><p>Remove square-root discriminants uniformly. Every ordinary endoscopic descendant has the same actual \\(F\\)-Levi \\(M\\). For \\(P_H=MU_H\\), put\n\\[\nR_{H,M}(Y)=\n\\det(\\operatorname{ad}(Y)|_{\\operatorname{Lie}U_H}).\n\\]\nOpposite roots give\n\\[\nD_H(Y)=(-1)^{\\dim U_H}D_M(Y)R_{H,M}(Y)^2.\n\\]\nDividing the specialized recursion by \\(|D_M(Y)|^{1/2}\\) therefore replaces every discriminant factor by \\(|R_{H,M}(Y)|\\), an integral valuation power. Paired Levi discriminants differ by a fixed rational scalar \\(u_\\psi\\); enlarge \\(N\\) to make \\(|u_\\psi|=1\\), so the removed factors agree.</p><p>The ordinary stable recursion is\n\\[\nS_M^H\n=\nJ_{M,M}^H\n-\n\\sum_{H'\\ne H}\\iota_M(H,H')S_M^{H'}.\n\\]\nIts sets and rational coefficients depend only on the fixed root data and cyclic action; descendants have fewer roots and the same Levi. The weighted integrands and hyperspecial indicators are the definable relatively integrable families of the cited transfer framework. Compatible weight-space constants are allowed. Differential-form and resultant comparisons preserve constructibility and relative integrability: the ratio of invariant orbit forms comes from the base and can be evaluated at the Kostant lift, so the projection formula applies.</p><p>Define modified recursions using \\(|R_{H,M}|\\) times quotient orbital integrals. Their specializations are \\(S_M^H/|D_M|^{1/2}\\), without adjoining square roots to the motivic ring. Expand the finite recursions and multiply by the product of ambient volumes, with multiplicities if necessary. The desired equality becomes\n\\[\n\\Phi=0\n\\]\nfor one constructible function on the enlarged common parameter space. Every specialized multiplier is positive.</p><p>By hypothesis, \\(\\Phi\\) vanishes identically over \\(k((t))\\) for every sufficiently large finite residue characteristic, including all generator variants. The transfer principle gives identical vanishing over every sufficiently large-residue-characteristic characteristic-zero local field with the same residue field.</p><p>Undo the positive volume multipliers and the common nonzero Levi-discriminant factor. The extension and frame parameterizations are surjective, so the normalized identity holds for every integral generically regular matching parameter. Taking the maximum of the finitely many bounds proves uniformity for the collection.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Lemma 2.3 and Proposition 2.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A_0/\\mathbf Q\\) be an elliptic curve, let \\(p&gt;2\\), and suppose\n  \\[\n  r=s_p(A_0)\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(N\\) for the conductor and\n  \\[\n  V=T_pA_0\\otimes_{\\mathbf Z_p}\\mathbf Q_p.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a number field \\(M\\), define\n  \\[\n  H_f^1(M_v,V)\n  =\n  \\operatorname{im}\\left(\n  A_0(M_v)^\\wedge_p\\otimes_{\\mathbf Z_p}\\mathbf Q_p\n  \\longrightarrow H^1(M_v,V)\n  \\right)\n  \\]\n  and impose these conditions globally to define \\(H_f^1(M,V)\\). Then\n  \\[\n  \\dim_{\\mathbf Q_p}H_f^1(M,V)\n  =\n  \\operatorname{corank}_{\\mathbf Z_p}\n  \\operatorname{Sel}_{p^\\infty}(A_0/M).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use the following external inputs, stated in the paper as Theorems 2.1 and 2.2:</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">1. For every elliptic curve \\(E/\\mathbf Q\\), a full \\(2\\)-power Selmer corank \\(r\\in\\{0,1\\}\\) implies analytic and Mordell–Weil rank \\(r\\) and finite \\(\\Sha(E/\\mathbf Q)\\).</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">2. Among nonzero squarefree integers \\(d\\), ordered by \\(|d|\\), the twists \\(E^{(d)}\\) of analytic rank zero and one each have density \\(1/2\\). Thus the twists of analytic rank at least two have density zero.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the \\(p\\)-parity theorem, modularity, and the Gross–Zagier–Kolyvagin theorems in analytic ranks zero and one.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are coprime negative odd fundamental discriminants \\(D,D'\\) as in Lemma 1.1 below. Put\n\\[\nK=\\mathbf Q(\\sqrt D),\\qquad\nF=\\mathbf Q(\\sqrt{DD'}),\\qquad\nL=\\mathbf Q(\\sqrt D,\\sqrt{D'}).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\dim H_f^1(K,V)=1,\n\\qquad\n\\dim H_f^1(K,V^{DD'})=1.\n\\]\nThe second line localizes nontrivially at both places above \\(p\\).</p><p>Let \\(y\\in A_0(K)\\) be the norm of the conductor-one Heegner point under a modular parametrization taking \\(\\infty\\) to zero. If \\(y\\) is non-torsion, the conclusion of Theorem 1 holds for \\(A_0,p,r\\).</p><p>For subsequent propositions, the odd-prime contradiction setting means these fixed data together with the assumption\n\\[\ny=0\\quad\\text{in }A_0(K)\\otimes\\mathbf Q.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The \\(p\\)-parity theorem gives\n\\[\nw(A_0)=(-1)^r.\n\\]\nFor a fundamental discriminant \\(d\\) prime to \\(N\\),\n\\[\nw(A_0^d)=w(A_0)\\chi_d(-N).\n\\]\nThe splitting conditions and negative signs of \\(D,D'\\) therefore give\n\\[\nw(A_0^D)=w(A_0^{D'})=-(-1)^r,\n\\qquad\nw(A_0^{DD'})=(-1)^r.\n\\]\nBy Lemma 1.1, their analytic ranks are respectively\n\\[\n1-r,\\quad 1-r,\\quad r.\n\\]\nGross–Zagier–Kolyvagin gives the same Mordell–Weil and full prime-power Selmer ranks and finite Tate–Shafarevich groups for these three twists.</p><p>Restriction and corestriction give\n\\[\nH_f^1(K,V)\n=\nH_f^1(\\mathbf Q,V)\\oplus H_f^1(\\mathbf Q,V^D),\n\\]\nand the analogous decomposition for \\(V^{DD'}\\). Both dimensions are therefore \\(r+(1-r)=1\\).</p><p>The second Selmer line comes from twists of known analytic rank at most one, so it is generated by a non-torsion global point. Its logarithm at either \\(p\\)-adic completion is nonzero: on a sufficiently small finite-index formal subgroup the logarithm is injective, so a point of zero logarithm would be torsion. This requires no reduction hypothesis.</p><p>Every prime dividing \\(N\\) splits in \\(K\\). The Gross–Zagier formula identifies nonzero height of \\(y\\) with simple vanishing of\n\\[\nL(A_0/K,s)=L(A_0,s)L(A_0^D,s).\n\\]\nSince the second factor has order \\(1-r\\), non-torsion of \\(y\\) implies \\(a(A_0)=r\\). The analytic rank-zero/one theorems then give rank \\(r\\) and finiteness of the entire \\(\\Sha(A_0/\\mathbf Q)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Retain \\(A_0,p,N\\) and the analytic twist-density input of Proposition 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are coprime negative odd fundamental discriminants \\(D,D'\\) such that:</p><p class=\"li\">every prime dividing \\(2Np\\) splits in both imaginary quadratic fields;</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\left(\\frac D{|D'|}\\right)=1\\);</p><p class=\"li\">each of \\(A_0^D,A_0^{D'},A_0^{DD'}\\) has the smallest analytic rank permitted by its sign;</p><p class=\"li\">\\(L/F\\) is CM and unramified at every finite prime;</p><p class=\"li\">\\(L\\) has only the roots of unity \\(\\{\\pm1\\}\\);</p><p class=\"li\">if \\(A_0\\) has CM, \\(L\\) does not contain its CM field.</p>", "proof": "<p>Negative odd fundamental discriminants in any admissible fixed congruence class have positive density. The splitting requirements at \\(2Np\\) impose finitely many such conditions. Exclude the finitely many unwanted quadratic fields and apply the twist-density theorem to choose \\(D\\) for which \\(A_0^D\\) has analytic rank at most one.</p><p>Keep \\(D\\) fixed. The requirements that \\(D'\\) be coprime to \\(D\\), split \\(2Np\\), and satisfy the Jacobi-symbol condition again contain admissible positive-density congruence classes. For example, impose\n\\[\nD'\\equiv-1\\pmod{|D|}\n\\]\nand \\(D'\\equiv1\\) modulo a sufficiently divisible modulus supported on \\(2Np\\). The moduli are coprime, and \\(|D'|\\equiv1\\pmod{|D|}\\) gives the required symbol.</p><p>Apply the density theorem to \\(A_0\\) and \\(A_0^D\\). The union of their exceptional sets has density zero, so \\(D'\\) can be chosen in the prescribed classes with both\n\\[\nA_0^{D'},\\qquad (A_0^D)^{D'}=A_0^{DD'}\n\\]\nof analytic rank at most one. Passing between squarefree parameters and fundamental discriminants preserves the density-zero assertion; in the odd classes used here the parameter is the discriminant.</p><p>The three quadratic subfields have discriminants \\(D,D',DD'\\). Hence\n\\[\n|\\operatorname{disc}(L)|=D^2(D')^2=\\operatorname{disc}(F)^2,\n\\]\nso the relative discriminant of \\(L/F\\) has norm one. This proves finite unramifiedness. The two imaginary subfields give the CM involution. The exclusions remove extra roots of unity and the possible CM field of \\(A_0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Proposition 3.7. Its inputs are Lemmas 3.1, 3.4–3.6 and Proposition 3.2; Definition 3.3 is included below", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work in the odd-prime contradiction setting of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(T=T_pA_0\\), \\(V=T\\otimes\\mathbf Q_p\\), and fix a sufficiently large finite extension \\(k/\\mathbf Q_p\\), with integers \\(\\mathcal O\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a conjugation-stable finite set \\(S\\) containing the places above \\(p\\), the bad places, and all fixed conductors and tests. Every additional enlargement is fixed before the final moving-prime sequence is realized.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a CM type \\(\\Sigma\\) induced from \\(K\\), its \\(p\\)-adic half \\(\\Sigma_p\\), and complex conjugation \\(c\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(\\epsilon\\) for the cyclotomic character. Geometric reciprocity is used except in the prime construction and cohomological tame-pairing calculation, where Frobenius is arithmetic.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix the algebraic character \\(\\mathcal C\\) of \\(U(1)_{K/\\mathbf Q}\\) of infinity type \\(s\\mapsto s\\), with fixed conductor on split primes away from \\(pDD'N\\), and use the same notation for its norm pullback and \\(p\\)-adic realization.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose moving primes, cyclic quotients, and logarithms\n  \\[\n  q_i,\\quad \\Delta_i\\simeq\\mathbf Z/p^{n_i}\\mathbf Z,\\quad\n  \\ell_i:G_K\\longrightarrow\\Delta_i\n  \\]\n  as in Lemma 2.2.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a nonprincipal ultrafilter \\(\\mathcal U\\). For \\(b\\ge1\\), let\n  \\[\n  R_b=k[t]/(t^b),\\qquad\n  \\ell((g_i))=\\lim_{\\mathcal U}\\ell_i(g_i)\\in\\mathbf Z_p,\n  \\qquad\n  \\psi=(1+t)^\\ell.\n  \\]\n  For \\(\\eta\\in\\{1,-1\\}\\), put\n  \\[\n  M_b^\\eta=V\\otimes_{\\mathbf Q_p}R_b(\\psi^\\eta).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">An admissible cochain is a coefficientwise ultralimit, in fixed bases, of continuous finite-stage cochains with one denominator \\(p^C\\) independent of \\(i\\). Cochain identities must hold modulo precisions \\(A_i\\to\\infty\\), with\n  \\[\n  A_i\\le n_i-c_b.\n  \\]\n  Equivalently, the limiting identity holds on every sequence of arguments. Coboundaries must also be admissible.</p><p class=\"li\">Over \\(L\\), \\(H_{\\mathrm{Gr}}^1(L,M_b^\\eta)\\) has full local condition at \\(\\Sigma_p\\), zero condition at its conjugates, relaxed conditions at the other places of \\(S\\), and the stipulated unramified conditions elsewhere.</p><p class=\"li\">The strict case is the remaining case in Lemma 2.1: \\(r=1\\), and the \\(V/K\\) compact Selmer line is everywhere locally zero.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For the prime sequence of Lemma 2.2, refined in the strict case as in Lemma 2.5, every \\(b\\ge1\\) and either sign satisfy\n\\[\n\\operatorname{length}_{R_b}H_{\\mathrm{Gr}}^1(L,V\\otimes\\psi^\\eta)\n=\n0\n\\quad\\text{outside the strict case},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\operatorname{length}_{R_b}H_{\\mathrm{Gr}}^1(L,V\\otimes\\psi^\\eta)\n\\le2\n\\quad\\text{in the strict case}.\n\\]\nIn the strict case, the module is killed by \\(t\\) and is the image of its two-dimensional base space under multiplication by \\(t^{b-1}\\).</p><p>Set\n\\[\nb_*=\n\\begin{cases}\n1,&amp;\\text{outside the strict case},\\\\\n3,&amp;\\text{in the strict case}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\operatorname{length}_{R_{b_*}}\nH_{\\mathrm{Gr}}^1(L,V\\otimes\\psi^\\eta)&lt;b_*.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Work in the two summands over \\(K\\). Let \\(S_b^\\bullet\\) be the cone, shifted by \\(-1\\), from global admissible cochains to the local complex at the strict \\(p\\)-place. Global and local \\(H^0\\) vanish, first for \\(V\\) and then by the \\(t\\)-filtration for \\(M_b^\\eta\\). Its degree-one cohomology is the Greenberg group.</p><p>The coefficient sequence of Lemma 2.3 gives\n\\[\n\\ker\\bigl(H^1(S_b^\\bullet)\\to H^1(S_1^\\bullet)\\bigr)\n=\n\\operatorname{im}\\bigl(t:H^1(S_{b-1}^\\bullet)\\to H^1(S_b^\\bullet)\\bigr).\n\\]\nNo flatness is assumed.</p><p>If the base group vanishes, induction gives vanishing at every order. Lemma 2.1 handles the auxiliary summand and both summands outside the strict case.</p><p>In the strict summand, every class at order \\(b\\ge2\\) reduces to an order-two class whose base specialization vanishes by Lemmas 2.4 and 2.5. Iterating the displayed identity gives\n\\[\nH^1(S_b^\\bullet)\n=\n\\operatorname{im}\\bigl(t^{b-1}:H^1(S_1^\\bullet)\\to H^1(S_b^\\bullet)\\bigr).\n\\]\nThis image is killed by \\(t\\) and has \\(k\\)-dimension at most two. Its \\(R_b\\)-length is at most two, proving all assertions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(W\\) be \\(V\\) or \\(V^{DD'}\\) over \\(K\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(v,v^c\\) be the places above \\(p\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H_{\\mathrm{rel}}^1(K,W)\\) have full conditions at both \\(p\\)-places.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H_{\\mathrm{Gr}}^1(K,W)\\) have full condition at \\(v\\) and zero at \\(v^c\\); let \\(H_{\\overline{\\mathrm{Gr}}}^1(K,W)\\) have the opposite conditions.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If the compact Selmer line of \\(W/K\\) has nonzero localization, then\n\\[\nH_{\\mathrm{Gr}}^1(K,W)\n=\nH_{\\overline{\\mathrm{Gr}}}^1(K,W)=0.\n\\]\nOtherwise, only the \\(V\\)-summand can occur, \\(r=1\\), and the compact Selmer line is generated by a class \\(a\\) with \\(a^c=a\\) and every localization zero. In this case\n\\[\nH_{\\mathrm{Gr}}^1(K,V)=\\langle a,h\\rangle,\n\\qquad\nH_{\\overline{\\mathrm{Gr}}}^1(K,V)=\\langle a,h^c\\rangle,\n\\]\nwhere \\(h\\) localizes nontrivially at \\(v\\) and trivially at \\(v^c\\). The \\(V^{DD'}\\)-summand always has zero Greenberg space.</p>", "proof": "<p>The alternating Weil pairing and the local invariant give a symmetric perfect pairing on \\(H^1(K_w,W)\\). At \\(w\\mid p\\), local torsion is finite, the rational Kummer space is a self-orthogonal line, and local Euler characteristic gives dimension two. Thus this is a hyperbolic plane for arbitrary reduction. At \\(w\\nmid p\\),\n\\[\nH^0(K_w,W)=H^2(K_w,W)=H^1(K_w,W)=0:\n\\]\nuse finite local torsion, local duality, and Euler characteristic.</p><p>Put \\(F_w=H_f^1(K_w,W)\\) and \\(Q_w=H^1(K_w,W)/F_w\\). Poitou–Tate identifies the image of\n\\[\nH_{\\mathrm{rel}}^1(K,W)\\longrightarrow Q_v\\oplus Q_{v^c}\n\\]\nwith the annihilator of the compact Selmer localization in \\(F_v\\oplus F_{v^c}\\).</p><p>If that localization is nonzero, both coordinates are nonzero, since conjugation preserves the global line and interchanges the places. Its annihilator is a line with both coordinates nonzero on each nonzero vector. A class zero at one place therefore has zero singular image, belongs to the compact Selmer line, and is killed by its zero local condition.</p><p>If the compact line is everywhere locally zero, the singular image is all of \\(Q_v\\oplus Q_{v^c}\\), and the kernel of localization is the compact line. Choose conjugation-compatible isotropic complements \\(E_w\\) of \\(F_w\\). The relaxed local image is the graph\n\\[\nE_v\\oplus E_{v^c}\\longrightarrow F_v\\oplus F_{v^c}.\n\\]\nReciprocity makes the graph isotropic; in dual bases its matrix is\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;u\\\\-u&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nConjugation interchanges both pairs and changes \\(u\\) to \\(-u\\), so \\(u=0\\). Lift the first coordinate to obtain \\(h\\), and conjugate to obtain \\(h^c\\).</p><p>The auxiliary line is generated by a non-torsion point and has nonzero localization by Proposition 1. If \\(r=0\\), the \\(V/K\\) line comes from a non-torsion point on \\(A_0^D/\\mathbf Q\\). If \\(r=1\\), the \\(D\\)-twist has zero Selmer space, so the strict line comes from \\(\\mathbf Q\\) and satisfies \\(a^c=a\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Proposition 3.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Retain the fixed data of Proposition 2. Frobenius in this lemma is arithmetic.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are rational primes \\(q_i\\notin S\\), split completely in \\(L\\), distinguished places \\(\\mathfrak q_i\\) of \\(K\\), cyclic \\(p\\)-quotients \\(\\Delta_i\\) of the ring class groups of conductor \\(q_i\\), and surjections\n\\[\n\\ell_i:G_K\\longrightarrow\\Delta_i\\simeq\\mathbf Z/p^{n_i}\\mathbf Z,\n\\qquad n_i\\to\\infty,\n\\]\nsuch that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(\\ell_i\\) is unramified outside the pair above \\(q_i\\), surjective on inertia at \\(\\mathfrak q_i\\), satisfies \\(\\ell_i^c=-\\ell_i\\), and is zero on every fixed decomposition group at \\(S\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. \\(v_p(q_i-1)-n_i\\) is bounded. At either \\(p\\)-place, the full conductor-\\(q_i\\) ring class field is unramified and its local degree has bounded \\(p\\)-part.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Frobenius on all fixed data unramified outside \\(S\\) tends to \\(\\gamma\\in G_L\\) with\n   \\[\n   \\epsilon(\\gamma)=1,\\qquad\n   \\det(V(\\gamma)-1)\\ne0,\\qquad\n   \\mathcal C(\\gamma)\\text{ not a root of unity}.\n   \\]\n   The element \\(\\gamma\\) fixes all \\(p\\)-power radicals of a fixed conjugation-stable finite list spanning\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal O_{K,S}^{\\times}\\otimes\\mathbf Q_p\n   \\]\n   under Kummer theory.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose ideal-class representatives prime to \\(S\\), principal generators for their orders, multiplication relations, and expressions of the primes in \\(S\\). Add generators of the \\(S\\)-unit group and their conjugates, obtaining \\(\\alpha_1,\\ldots,\\alpha_d\\). Class-group orders and roots of unity cost only a fixed index.</p><p>For a split prime \\(q\\), the \\(p\\)-part of the kernel from the ring class group to the ideal class group is cyclic of order \\(p^{v_p(q-1)}\\), up to a fixed factor. Require the \\(\\alpha_j\\) to have zero residue logarithm modulo \\(p^{n_i}\\), by splitting in their radical extensions and the corresponding cyclotomic fields. The ring-class presentation then permits zero logarithms on the ideal representatives and the surjective logarithm on the residue quotient. Conjugation inverts this quotient, and the chosen relations give the required vanishing at \\(S\\).</p><p>Over the cyclotomic tower, the Kummer group is a closed subgroup of \\(\\mathbf Z_p(1)^d\\). A common Lie quotient with the joint image of \\(V\\) and \\(\\mathcal C\\) is abelian, hence kills the derived Lie algebra of the image of \\(V\\). On the remaining torus Lie algebra an open cyclotomic subgroup acts trivially, whereas on Kummer translations it acts by the cyclotomic character. The equivariant common quotient is zero. Killing the radicals therefore removes no Lie dimension from the joint image over the cyclotomic tower.</p><p>For non-CM \\(A_0\\), the determinant-one image has Lie algebra \\(\\mathfrak{sl}_2\\); for CM \\(A_0\\), the identity component contains the norm-one torus. In either case \\(\\det(V(g)-1)\\ne0\\) is nonempty and open in every sufficiently small identity neighborhood. The infinity type of \\(\\mathcal C\\) makes its restriction over the cyclotomic tower nonconstant; its torsion locus is proper there. Choose \\(\\gamma\\) in the intersection, also imposing membership in \\(G_L\\) and any fixed open-kernel conditions.</p><p>To control \\(v_p(q_i-1)\\), use the cyclotomic–radical image in\n\\[\n\\mathbf Z_p(1)^d\\rtimes\\mathbf Z_p^\\times.\n\\]\nChoose a pro-\\(p\\) element with nontorsion cyclotomic projection. Its \\(p^m\\)-th powers have translation coordinates divisible by \\(p^{m-C_0}\\), and cyclotomic valuation \\(m+C_1\\). Multiplying \\(\\gamma\\) by such a power with \\(m=n_i+C_2\\) preserves the radical conditions to the requested precision and gives cyclotomic valuation exactly \\(n_i+C\\). Chebotarev in increasing finite quotients realizes these conditions and the approximations to fixed data.</p><p>The full ring class group has \\(p\\)-part \\(p^{v_p(q_i-1)+O(1)}\\). Its kernel over \\(\\Delta_i\\) thus has bounded \\(p\\)-part. Frobenius at each fixed place belongs to this kernel. At \\(p\\) the extension is unramified, so the \\(p\\)-part of the local degree is bounded.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "Lemma 3.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the admissible complexes and coefficient modules of Proposition 2. For every fixed \\(b\\), the binomial-polynomial estimate gives a constant \\(c_b\\) such that\n\\[\nu\\equiv u'\\pmod{p^n}\n\\Longrightarrow\n\\binom uj\\equiv\\binom{u'}j\\pmod{p^{n-c_b}},\n\\qquad 0\\le j&lt;b.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For constant coefficients \\(V\\), admissible \\(H^1\\) is ordinary continuous \\(H^1(G_{K,S},V)\\), and likewise over \\(L\\). For \\(\\mathbf Q_p(1)\\), the same holds when unramifiedness at the moving primes is imposed. Fixed local conditions specialize to the usual rational local conditions.</p><p>There are short exact sequences of admissible cochain complexes\n\\[\n0\\longrightarrow C^\\bullet(M_{b-1}^\\eta)\n\\xrightarrow{\\,t\\,}C^\\bullet(M_b^\\eta)\n\\longrightarrow C^\\bullet(V\\otimes k)\n\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nZero local conditions at \\(p\\) and local Kummer conditions are preserved when a class of zero specialization is divided by \\(t\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a stable lattice in \\(V\\). A bounded finite-stage cocycle modulo \\(p^{A_i}\\) has trivial wild inertia at \\(q_i\\). The tame Frobenius relation gives\n\\[\n(V(\\operatorname{Fr}_i)-q_i)z_i(\\tau)=0\n\\pmod{p^{A_i-C}}.\n\\]\nThe inverse matrices have a fixed denominator because they converge to \\((V(\\gamma)-1)^{-1}\\). After one fixed precision loss, the cocycle kills the normal subgroup generated by moving inertia and factors through \\(G_{K,S}\\). The same holds at the conjugate prime and over \\(L\\).</p><p>For any fixed finite coefficient module there are only finitely many continuous cocycles on \\(G_{K,S}\\): use finiteness of bounded-degree extensions with fixed ramification and finiteness of coboundaries. Compatible ultralimits therefore give an actual continuous integral cocycle. Undo the scaling. For cyclotomic coefficients, impose moving unramifiedness first. Bounded zero-cochains have compact ultralimits, so classes and coboundaries are compatible.</p><p>Exactness of the coefficient sequence is coefficientwise: lift finitely many continuous coordinates, and divide a zero constant coordinate by shifting coordinates. Only the fixed binomial loss occurs.</p><p>The tame character is exactly trivial at fixed places, so\n\\[\nH^1(K_s,M_b^\\eta)=H^1(K_s,V\\otimes k)\\otimes_kR_b,\n\\]\nand similarly for \\(H_f^1\\). These are fixed linear subspaces. Since \\(H^0(K_s,V)=0\\), finitely many \\(g_j\\) make\n\\[\nA\\longmapsto((V(g_j)-1)A)_j\n\\]\ninjective. A fixed left inverse bounds all local coboundary corrections. Thus strict and Kummer conditions survive division, including in finite-precision representatives.</p><p>Finally, restriction, corestriction, and the two idempotents for \\(\\operatorname{Gal}(L/K)\\) decompose the Greenberg spaces into the \\(V\\) and \\(V^{DD'}\\) summands over \\(K\\), with fixed denominators.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.4", "orig": "Lemma 3.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Assume the strict case.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose conjugation-equivariant continuous cocycles for \\(a,h,h^c\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Contract cup products using the Weil pairing \\(e:V\\times V\\to\\mathbf Q_p(1)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For\n  \\[\n  x\\in H_{\\mathrm{Gr}}^1(K,V),\\qquad\n  d\\in H_{\\overline{\\mathrm{Gr}}}^1(K,V),\n  \\]\n  choose a continuous primitive \\(u\\) with\n  \\[\n  \\delta u=x\\cup d.\n  \\]\n  Choose primitives bilinearly on fixed bases.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  z_\\gamma(x,d;u)\n  =\n  u(\\gamma)-\n  e\\bigl((V(\\gamma)-1)^{-1}x(\\gamma),d(\\gamma)\\bigr),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  H_\\gamma(x,d)\n  =\n  z_\\gamma(x,d;u)-z_\\gamma(x^c,d^c;u^c).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The pairing \\(H_\\gamma\\) is independent of the primitive and is bilinear. If a nonzero base class \\(x\\) is the specialization of an admissible class in\n\\[\nH_{\\mathrm{Gr}}^1(K,M_2^\\eta),\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\nH_\\gamma(x,d)=0\n\\quad\\text{for every }d\\in H_{\\overline{\\mathrm{Gr}}}^1(K,V\\otimes k).\n\\]\nThis necessary condition holds for either \\(\\eta=1\\) or \\(\\eta=-1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The cup product vanishes locally everywhere: at \\(p\\), one class has zero local restriction, and away from \\(p\\), rational \\(H^1\\) vanishes. Brauer reciprocity therefore gives the primitive. The finitely many chosen primitives have one fixed denominator.</p><p>For \\(\\eta=1\\), write a first-order lift as \\(x+tx_i'\\). Lemma 2.3 allows the constant coefficient to be replaced by the fixed representative using bounded coboundaries and uniformly vanishing errors. The linear equation is\n\\[\n\\delta x_i'=-\\ell_i\\cup x.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\nx_i'\\cup d-\\ell_i\\cup u\n\\]\nis a two-cocycle to increasing precision.</p><p>Its invariants vanish at \\(S\\): at \\(p\\), use opposite strict conditions and \\(\\ell_i=0\\); away from \\(p\\), use rational vanishing and bounded exponents of fixed integral local cohomology. Outside \\(Sq_i\\), it is unramified with zero invariant.</p><p>At \\(\\mathfrak q_i\\), write\n\\[\nx=\\delta A_i,\\qquad d=\\delta B_i,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nA_i=(V(\\operatorname{Fr}_i)-1)^{-1}x(\\operatorname{Fr}_i),\n\\quad\nB_i=(V(\\operatorname{Fr}_i)-1)^{-1}d(\\operatorname{Fr}_i).\n\\]\nThese have bounded denominators. The local invariant is minus that of\n\\[\n\\ell_i\\cup(u-A_i\\cup d).\n\\]\nThe second factor is unramified. The tame pairing evaluates it on Frobenius with a unit multiplier, since the inertia logarithm is surjective. At the conjugate prime the multiplier is the same and \\(\\ell_i^c=-\\ell_i\\). The global sum of invariants gives\n\\[\n\\nu H_\\gamma(x,d)=0,\\qquad \\nu\\in\\mathbf Z_p^\\times.\n\\]</p><p>All reductions can be made at\n\\[\nN_i\\le\\min\\{n_i,A_i,B_i\\}-C,\\qquad N_i\\to\\infty,\n\\]\nwhere here \\(A_i\\) and \\(B_i\\) denote the available identity and replacement precisions, and \\(C\\) bounds the finitely many fixed cocycles, primitives, local inverses, integral local cohomology exponents, and binomial losses. No division by \\(q_i-1\\) occurs. For \\(\\eta=-1\\), replace \\(\\ell_i\\) by \\(-\\ell_i\\).</p><p>Two primitives differ by a class in\n\\[\nH^1(G_{K,S},\\mathbf Q_p(1))\n=\n\\mathcal O_{K,S}^{\\times}\\otimes\\mathbf Q_p.\n\\]\nEvery such Kummer class evaluates to zero at \\(\\gamma\\), by the radical condition; a coboundary does also because \\(\\epsilon(\\gamma)=1\\). This proves independence. Bilinearity follows from the fixed bilinear choices.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.5", "orig": "Lemma 3.6", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Assume the strict case and use the pairing of Lemma 2.4.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The element \\(\\gamma\\) in Lemma 2.2 can be chosen so that, in the bases\n\\[\n(a,h),\\qquad(a,h^c),\n\\]\nthe obstruction matrix is\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n0&amp;\\kappa\\\\\n-\\kappa&amp;0\n\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad \\kappa\\in\\mathbf Q_p^\\times.\n\\]\nThus no nonzero base Greenberg class lifts to order two, for either sign.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(q(g)=e(x(g),d(g))\\). The cocycle equations give\n\\[\n\\delta q=d\\cup x-x\\cup d.\n\\]\nThus \\(u+q\\) is a primitive for the switched product. The difference of the corrected evaluations is\n\\[\n(\\epsilon(\\gamma)-1)e(A,B)=0.\n\\]\nConsequently \\(H_\\gamma(x,d)=H_\\gamma(d,x)\\), while\n\\[\nH_\\gamma(x^c,d^c)=-H_\\gamma(x,d).\n\\]\nSince \\(a^c=a\\) and \\(p&gt;2\\), the matrix has the asserted skew form.</p><p>Set \\(d^-=h^c-h\\), and choose an anti-invariant primitive for \\(a\\cup d^-\\). Then\n\\[\nH_\\gamma(a,d^-)=2z_\\gamma(a,d^-;u)=2H_\\gamma(a,h^c).\n\\]\nIt remains to make the corrected evaluation nonzero.</p><p>Let\n\\[\nG=G_{K,S},\\qquad N_V=\\ker(G\\to\\operatorname{GL}(V)).\n\\]\nThe classes \\(a,d^-\\) are independent. The image on \\(V\\) contains a central scalar different from one; the central-scalar cocycle identity makes\n\\[\nH^1(\\operatorname{im}(G),V)=0.\n\\]\nRestriction to \\(N_V\\) is therefore injective. The restrictions of \\(a,d^-\\) are additive homomorphisms, and their joint image spans \\(V\\oplus V\\). Otherwise absolute irreducibility and \\(\\operatorname{End}_G(V)=\\mathbf Q_p\\) would impose a scalar relation, contradicting injectivity. In the CM case, the choice that neither \\(K\\) nor \\(L\\) contains the CM field supplies the required irreducibility. Passage to \\(N_V\\cap G_L\\) does not change the span.</p><p>On \\(N_V\\), \\(\\epsilon=1\\) and\n\\[\nu(gg')=u(g)+u(g')-e(a(g),d^-(g')).\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\nu([g,g'])\n=\n-e(a(g),d^-(g'))+e(a(g'),d^-(g)).\n\\]\nThe alternating form on \\(V\\oplus V\\) is nonzero and nondegenerate. Choose \\(g,g'\\in N_V\\cap G_L\\) with nonzero right side and set \\(\\sigma=[g,g']\\). Both translations vanish on \\(\\sigma\\), but \\(u(\\sigma)\\ne0\\).</p><p>Kummer characters are additive on \\(N_V\\), so kill \\(\\sigma\\); \\(\\mathcal C\\) kills it as well. Replacing \\(\\gamma\\) by \\(\\gamma\\sigma^m\\) preserves its \\(V,\\mathcal C\\), cyclotomic, and radical values, while changing the primitive evaluation by \\(mu(\\sigma)\\). Choose \\(m\\) to make it nonzero. A nonzero power of \\(\\sigma\\) preserves any further fixed open conditions.</p><p>Reselect the prime sequence by Lemma 2.2 for this refined \\(\\gamma\\), and form all admissible complexes from that sequence.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Proposition 3.9", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the odd-prime contradiction setting and final prime sequence of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H_i/K\\) be the full ring class field of conductor \\(q_i\\), let\n  \\[\n  G_i=\\operatorname{Gal}(H_i/K),\n  \\]\n  and let \\(y_i\\in A_0(H_i)\\) be the corresponding Heegner point.</p><p class=\"li\">At \\(q_i\\), use the conductor-\\(q_i\\) translate \\(n(1/q_i)\\) in torus-adapted coordinates, with the opposite sign if required by the embedding convention.</p><p class=\"li\">View \\(\\ell_i\\) as a quotient logarithm on \\(G_i\\). All sums are raw sums, without division by \\(|G_i|\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use \\(b_*\\) from Proposition 2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At the distinguished place above \\(p\\), for either sign,\n\\[\n\\sum_{g\\in G_i}(1+t)^{\\pm\\ell_i(g)}\n\\log_{A_0,p}(y_i^g)\n\\longrightarrow0\n\\pmod{t^{b_*}}.\n\\]\nEvery coefficient below degree \\(b_*\\) has valuation tending to \\(+\\infty\\) along \\(\\mathcal U\\). Coefficients through any other fixed degree have a common lower valuation bound in the fixed \\(p\\)-adic algebraic closure.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose integral Kummer cocycles for \\(y_i\\), corestrict with tautological group-ring coefficients, and truncate by the binomial substitution. Corestriction is a sum, so the resulting admissible classes have bounded denominators independently of \\(|G_i|\\) and satisfy Kummer conditions at both \\(p\\)-places.</p><p>The split Heegner norm relation makes their augmentation the Kummer class of\n\\[\n(a_{q_i}-2)y.\n\\]\nIndeed, the \\(q_i+1\\) cyclic subgroups in the Hecke correspondence consist of two horizontal CM isogenies and \\(q_i-1\\) descending isogenies. The latter form the conductor-\\(q_i\\) orbit. Tracing to \\(K\\), both horizontal terms become \\(y\\), and the Hecke action is \\(a_{q_i}\\). Since \\(y\\) is fixed torsion, one fixed scalar kills all augmentations.</p><p>For \\(b_*=1\\), this proves the cohomological assertion.</p><p>In the strict case, divide once by \\(t\\) using Proposition 2. The divided class is unique because multiplication by \\(t\\) is injective on \\(H^1\\), using \\(H^0(K,V\\otimes k)=0\\). Its specialization \\(z_1\\) is an ordinary compact Selmer class.</p><p>In fractional-ideal CM coordinates,\n\\[\nx(\\mathfrak a,\\mathfrak n)\n=\n(\\mathbf C/\\mathfrak a\\to\\mathbf C/\\mathfrak n^{-1}\\mathfrak a),\n\\qquad\n\\mathfrak n\\overline{\\mathfrak n}=(N),\n\\]\nthe dual isogeny and conjugation give\n\\[\nw_Nx(\\mathfrak a,\\mathfrak n)\n=\nx(\\mathfrak n^{-1}\\mathfrak a,\\overline{\\mathfrak n}),\n\\qquad\nx(\\mathfrak a,\\mathfrak n)^c\n=\nw_Nx(\\mathfrak n\\overline{\\mathfrak a},\\mathfrak n).\n\\]\nThus conjugation is inversion followed by a fixed translation. The modular parametrization satisfies the Fricke relation up to a fixed torsion cusp image. In the strict case \\(r=1\\), the Fricke scalar is \\(+1\\). Conjugation acts on the tame parameter by\n\\[\nt\\longmapsto(1+t)^{-1}-1=-t+t^2-\\cdots.\n\\]\nThe translating group-ring unit has augmentation one and does not affect the leading linear coefficient because the constant coefficient has vanished. Therefore\n\\[\nz_1^c=-z_1.\n\\]\nThe compact Selmer line is generated by invariant \\(a\\), so \\(z_1=0\\).</p><p>Divide once more. The specialization \\(z_2\\) again belongs to the compact Selmer line, which is everywhere locally zero. Hence the original trace has zero local Kummer class through degree two.</p><p>At \\(p\\), the fields \\(H_i\\) are unramified with local degrees of bounded \\(p\\)-part by Proposition 2. Restriction to the local field of definition makes the twisted Kummer trace the Kummer class of the raw weighted sum, to the allowed precision. Apply Lemma 3.1 after one fixed denominator-clearing scalar. The vanishing Kummer precisions tend to infinity, so the corresponding logarithm coefficients tend to zero. All coefficients through any fixed higher degree remain bounded by the same finite-truncation argument.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.8", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Fix \\(d_0\\ge0\\), and let \\(E/\\mathbf Q_p\\) vary over finite unramified extensions with\n\\[\nv_p([E:\\mathbf Q_p])\\le d_0.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a constant \\(c\\), independent of \\(E\\), such that\n\\[\n\\log_{A_0,p}(A_0(E))\\subseteq p^{-c}\\mathcal O_E.\n\\]\nIf \\(P\\in A_0(E)\\) and the Kummer class of \\(p^CP\\) vanishes in\n\\[\nH^1(E,A_0[p^n]),\n\\qquad C,n\\ge0,\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\nv_p(\\log_{A_0,p}P)\\ge n-C-c.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>On a fixed sufficiently small formal subgroup, logarithm and inverse are integral uniformly under unramified extension. The intervening formal quotient has bounded \\(p\\)-exponent. The geometric component group has fixed order; the additive part of the Néron special fiber has bounded \\(p\\)-exponent; a torus in characteristic \\(p\\) has no \\(p\\)-torsion.</p><p>For the étale \\(p\\)-power torsion of the abelian part, let \\(\\alpha\\) be a unit Frobenius eigenvalue. It is not a root of unity by its complex absolute value. Writing its torsion and principal-unit parts gives\n\\[\nv_p(\\alpha^d-1)\\le C_\\alpha+v_p(d)\n\\]\nwhenever the left side is positive. Thus the relevant exponent is bounded for \\(d=[E:\\mathbf Q_p]\\).</p><p>A fixed \\(p\\)-power, followed if needed by an integer prime to \\(p\\), moves a point into the formal subgroup. Divide the resulting logarithm by this multiplier to obtain the uniform bound.</p><p>The Kummer injection\n\\[\nA_0(E)/p^nA_0(E)\\hookrightarrow H^1(E,A_0[p^n])\n\\]\ngives \\(p^CP=p^nQ\\). Apply the uniform bound to \\(Q\\). No division by the local extension degree occurs.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Lemma 7.3, with geometric inputs Lemmas 7.1 and 7.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G=U(B\\oplus\\mathbb H)\\) have signature \\((3,1)\\) at both real places of \\(F\\), with the totally split good-prime PEL datum used in the paper.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose neat tame level and a fixed depth \\(s\\) at \\(p\\). Enlarge the fixed coefficient field to contain the necessary \\(p^s\\)-th roots of unity.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X^{\\mathrm{tor}},X^{\\min}\\) be compatible hyperspecial integral compactifications, \\(D\\) the reduced toroidal boundary, and\n  \\[\n  \\pi:Y^{\\mathrm{tor}}\\to X^{\\mathrm{tor}}\n  \\]\n  the finite normalization in generic level \\(J\\).</p><p class=\"li\">At a distinguished \\(p\\)-adic factor, \\(J\\) has upper block pattern \\((2,1,1)\\): a framed rank-two subflag inside a rank-three flag, full upper radical and last unit factors, and the prescribed fixed lower depths. It does not choose lifts of the étale quotient.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal H=\\omega^+\\), \\(d=m-1\\), and\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal D_d(\\mathcal H)\n  =\n  \\left(\\operatorname{Sym}^d((\\bigwedge^2\\mathcal H)^\\vee)\\right)^\\vee\n  =\n  \\Gamma^d(\\bigwedge^2\\mathcal H).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For weights used in the construction, define \\(\\mathcal E_\\lambda\\), at each distinguished embedding, by the positive factor\n  \\[\n  (\\det\\omega^+)^{\\lambda_3}\\otimes\\mathcal D_d(\\omega^+)\n  \\]\n  and the opposite differential line to exponent \\(-\\lambda_4\\). Put\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal M_\\lambda\n  =\n  (Y^{\\mathrm{tor}}\\to X^{\\min})_*\n  \\bigl(\\mathcal E_\\lambda(-\\pi^*D)\\bigr).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>An ordinary section modulo \\(p^M\\) on the canonical branch, with vanishing constant terms and extended by zero on the other branches, is a section of\n\\[\n\\mathcal M_\\lambda/p^M\n\\]\nover the ordinary open. The assertion holds with coefficients in\n\\[\n\\mathcal O[t]/(p^M,t^b)\n\\]\nand after finite extension of coefficients.</p>", "proof": "<p>The boundary has rank one over \\(L\\), and its residual binary Shimura datum is zero dimensional. Cusp tuples are indexed by strictly positive \\(\\alpha\\in F\\). A nonzero element of \\(F\\) cannot have one real embedding zero, so there are no nonzero rational indices on the boundary of the positive cone. Vanishing constant terms impose positive order along every relevant cone ray.</p><p>For \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\), the coefficient lies on an abelian extension-data chart \\(C\\) in\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_\\alpha\\otimes\\mathcal E_\\lambda|_C,\n\\]\nwith \\(\\mathcal L_\\alpha\\) ample. This persists on the finite étale abelian covers of Lemma 4.1. The semiabelian differential sequence has constant locally free graded pieces; Lemma 4.2 supplies a corresponding filtration of \\(\\mathcal E_\\lambda|_C\\).</p><p>An ample line bundle \\(\\mathcal L\\) on an abelian variety has no higher cohomology in every residue characteristic: choose multiplication by \\(n\\) prime to the characteristic, whose trace splits \\(\\mathcal O\\) inside \\([n]_*\\mathcal O\\). Then \\(H^r(\\mathcal L)\\) is a summand of \\(H^r([n]^*\\mathcal L)\\). For large \\(n\\), Serre vanishing, uniformly over algebraically trivial twists by properness of the dual abelian variety, kills the latter. Apply the filtration to obtain the same vanishing after tensoring with \\(\\mathcal E_\\lambda|_C\\).</p><p>Cohomology and base change lifts each positive coefficient integrally. The arithmetic stabilizer of a positive index has norm one; the CM norm-one unit group and binary definite stabilizer are finite, and neatness removes them. Lift one coefficient in each free orbit and transport it, without averaging.</p><p>The tuples converge on every formal cone: an interior rational ray defines a functional whose bounded values bound both real coordinates, leaving finitely many lattice indices. Formal charts and formal functions identify the tuples with completed pushforward sections. Injectivity follows from left exactness of pushforward on multiplication by a uniformizer. Faithfulness of completion gives the assertion on the ordinary open. The branch decomposition permits zero on other branches, and the Artin-ring assertion follows coefficientwise because \\(\\mathcal O[t]/(t^b)\\) is finite free.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "Lemma 7.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the normalization and level \\(J\\) of Proposition 4. At each distinguished factor, the ordinary \\(p\\)-divisible group has multiplicative height three and étale height one.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Over the ordinary formal completion, the locus where the rank-three flag equals the multiplicative subgroup is an open and closed branch of \\(Y^{\\mathrm{tor}}\\). The remaining flag and frame data are finite étale data on its Cartier-dual character group. On a toroidal cusp chart, this branch adds finite étale covers of the abelian extension-data scheme and does not ramify the center Fourier variable.</p>", "proof": "<p>The ordinary group has a canonical multiplicative subgroup at every fixed level \\(p^s\\). Once the rank-three flag is this subgroup, Cartier duality identifies its rank-two flag and frame with quotients and markings of a finite étale character group. The full upper radical ensures that no splitting of the étale quotient is specified. The corresponding generic idempotents extend in the finite normalization over the ordinary completion; excellence permits normalization after completion.</p><p>At a rank-one boundary, the Raynaud semiabelian group is an extension of the binary abelian group by a rank-one torus. The preimage of binary multiplicative torsion is an extension of multiplicative-type groups, hence is multiplicative type. This is the distinguished rank-three multiplicative subgroup. It is constructed before quotienting by the period lattice and is independent of the center Fourier parameter.</p><p>Over a completed strict boundary base, a pointed connected finite étale cover of an abelian chart is an abelian scheme and the covering is an isogeny. On geometric fibers, lift addition through the abelian fundamental group; unique pointed lifts give the group laws. Henselian thickening extends the construction.</p><p>Every relevant base component has an ordinary cusp, and every canonical finite étale component meets the corresponding cusp charts. Thus reduced ordinary expansions detect vertical divisibility, successively modulo all uniformizer powers. Boundary representatives and prime-to-\\(p\\) Jacobi torsions can be chosen integral at \\(p,q_i\\), and every fixed exceptional place by Iwasawa decomposition and approximation in the cusp parabolic and its binary Cayley chart.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "Lemma 7.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(\\mathcal H\\) be finite locally free of rank three and use \\(\\mathcal D_d\\) from Proposition 4.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The functor \\(\\mathcal D_d\\) is finite locally free, commutes with base change, and preserves its lattice under integral Hodge-frame changes. A diagonal map with entries \\(c_1,c_2,c_3\\) acts in the literal monomial-dual basis by\n\\[\n(c_1c_2)^{a_{12}}(c_1c_3)^{a_{13}}(c_2c_3)^{a_{23}},\n\\qquad\na_{12}+a_{13}+a_{23}=d.\n\\]\nA subbundle filtration of \\(\\mathcal H\\) with constant locally free graded pieces induces one on \\(\\mathcal D_d(\\mathcal H)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the integral definition\n\\[\n\\Gamma^d(W)=\\left(\\operatorname{Sym}^d(W^\\vee)\\right)^\\vee\n\\]\nwith \\(W=\\bigwedge^2\\mathcal H\\). A minor monomial pairs to one with its literal dual, without a factorial. Finite local freeness, base change, frame covariance, and the diagonal formula follow directly, including for nonunit \\(c_j\\).</p><p>Locally split a filtration of \\(\\mathcal H\\). Exterior and symmetric powers have their degree filtrations; dualizing gives the divided-power filtration. Block-triangular transition maps preserve it, so it descends. Its graded pieces are constant when those of \\(\\mathcal H\\) are constant. No rational splitting is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Proposition 4.3 and the bounded fixed-place spanning assertion of Lemma 6.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain \\(K,F,L,p,k,\\mathcal C\\) and the moving tame quotients of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(\\delta=\\sqrt D\\) positive imaginary for \\(\\Sigma\\), and\n  \\[\n  A=(L^2,1_2),\\qquad\n  W=B\\oplus\\mathbb H,\\qquad\n  B=(L^2,b_0\\delta\\,1_2),\n  \\]\n  with \\(b_0\\in F^{\\times,+}\\) a \\(p\\)-unit satisfying\n  \\[\n  v(b_0\\delta\\mathfrak d_F)\\equiv0\\pmod2\n  \\]\n  at every nonsplit finite place.</p><p class=\"li\">Use matched self-dual lattices, trivial even splitting characters, and the trace additive character.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\pi\\) on compact \\(U(A)\\) be obtained from the weight-two form of \\(A_0\\) by real quadratic base change, Jacquet–Langlands transfer, and pullback. Its real component and adelic central character are trivial. Its base change \\(\\pi_L\\) is cuspidal.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\nu_i\\) be the universal character of \\(\\Delta_i\\), pulled back by \\(N_{L/K}\\), and set\n  \\[\n  \\eta=\\mathcal C^m\\nu_i,\\qquad m&gt;0.\n  \\]\n  The integers \\(m\\) tend to infinity ordinarily and to zero \\(p\\)-adically on a fixed branch killing the relevant finite torsion orders.</p><p class=\"li\">The theta integral is\n  \\[\n  \\Theta_{i,m}(g;\\Phi,z)\n  =\n  \\int_{[U(A)]}\\theta_\\Phi(g,h)\\,\n  z(h)\\eta^{-1}(\\det h)\\,dh.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">At infinity use Fock polynomials of degree \\(m-1\\) in the \\(2\\times2\\) minors of the \\(2\\times3\\) positive-coordinate matrix.</p><p class=\"li\">In differential frames \\(\\mathcal H=\\omega^+\\), use\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal P_d(\\mathcal H)=\\operatorname{Sym}^d((\\bigwedge^2\\mathcal H)^\\vee),\n  \\qquad\n  \\mathcal D_d(\\mathcal H)=\\mathcal P_d(\\mathcal H)^\\vee,\n  \\quad d=m-1.\n  \\]\n  Coefficients use the literal dual basis to Fock monomials.</p><p class=\"li\">At a distinguished \\(p\\)-place, with columns \\((e,B_1,B_2,f)\\), use\n  \\[\n  \\Phi_p(Y)=\n  \\varphi_p(Y_{[1,2]})1_{\\mathbf Z_p^2}(Y_3)1_{\\mathbf Z_p^2}(Y_4),\n  \\qquad\n  \\operatorname{supp}\\varphi_p\\subseteq\\operatorname{GL}_2(\\mathbf Z_p),\n  \\]\n  at fixed depth and level \\(J\\).</p><p class=\"li\">At each place above \\(q_i\\), use\n  \\[\n  1_{\\operatorname{GL}_2(\\mathcal O_q)}(Y_{[1,2]})\n  \\eta_q(\\det Y_{[1,2]})\n  1_{M_{2,2}(\\mathcal O_q)}(Y_{[3,4]}).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Position moments are first defined in fixed Igusa differential frames. Fixed algebraic unit-scale powers are restored afterward, or the polynomial norm is taken on the actual scaled support.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For all sufficiently large permitted \\(m\\), the construction gives holomorphic algebraic forms of weight\n\\[\n\\lambda=(m,m,1;-1)\n\\]\nwith uniformly bounded denominators, coefficientwise in the tame group algebra and after every fixed \\(R_b\\)-substitution. At split good spherical places their unitary standard parameter is\n\\[\n(\\pi\\otimes\\eta)\\oplus|\\cdot|^{-1/2}\\oplus|\\cdot|^{1/2}.\n\\]\nAt both distinguished \\(p\\)-places,\n\\[\np^{-2}U_2\\Theta_{i,m}=\\Theta_{i,m},\n\\qquad\np^{-2}U_3\\Theta_{i,m}=\\Theta_{i,m},\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nU_j=[Jt_jJ],\\qquad\nt_j=\\operatorname{diag}(1_j,p^{-1}1_{4-j}).\n\\]</p><p>At each fixed split place away from \\(p\\), the permitted upper-\\((2,2)\\) tests, or \\((3,1)\\)-parahoric tests in the indicated unramified Steinberg case, span any fixed finite collection of original tests in theta coinvariants after inverting a Laurent polynomial nonzero at \\(\\eta=1\\). Along weights tending to zero, finitely many translates and one bounded denominator suffice. These replacements leave the \\(p\\)- and \\(q_i\\)-tests unchanged.</p>", "proof": "<p>The prescribed \\(b_0\\) exists by the quadratic norm-invariant sequence: the possible odd local contributions have even total parity because\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac D{|D'|}\\right)=1.\n\\]\nThe real prescriptions are positive, and multiplication by a global norm makes \\(b_0\\) a \\(p\\)-unit without changing local classes. Matched self-dual lattices follow.</p><p>The compact source is the pullback from the quaternion algebra over \\(F\\) ramified at the two real places and no finite place. Successive base changes remain cuspidal because the possible inducing CM field was excluded.</p><p>The Fock vacuum contributes source determinant weight one; each minor adds one, giving source type \\(\\det^m\\). On the target the vacuum has weight \\((1,1,1;-1)\\), and the polynomial contributes \\((m-1,m-1,0;0)\\). Hence the weight is \\((m,m,1;-1)\\). Antiholomorphic lowering contractions involve absent negative coordinates and annihilate the vector.</p><p>Under a differential-frame change \\(e\\mapsto eg\\), creation frames change by \\(g^{-t}\\). Theta evaluation is linear in the Fock polynomial, so its coefficient array is the literal dual lattice\n\\[\n\\det(\\mathcal H)\\otimes\\Gamma^{m-1}(\\bigwedge^2\\mathcal H),\n\\]\nwith the opposite line to exponent one. No factorial enters. The PEL similitude model realizes these bundles on the isometry components or finite covers with compatible constants.</p><p>Lemma 5.1 gives the two exact \\(p\\)-operator values. Lemma 5.2 gives the spherical parameter and the fixed-place spanning statement. Lemma 5.4 gives holomorphy at cusps, algebraicity, and the uniform bounds. These arguments permit arbitrary fixed bounded split tests of the stipulated levels.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the split action\n\\[\n\\omega(h,g)\\Phi(Y)\n=\n|\\det h|^2|\\det g|\\,\\Phi({}^thYg)\n\\]\nand the \\(p\\)-tests of Proposition 5.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nU_2\\Phi_p=p^2\\Phi_p,\\qquad U_3\\Phi_p=p^2\\Phi_p.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Upper shifts are indexed by \\(M_{j,4-j}(\\mathbf F_p)\\). The expanding columns must become divisible by \\(p\\). Since the first two columns have rank two modulo \\(p\\), the equations have\n\\[\np^{(j-2)(4-j)}\n\\]\nsolutions on the original support and none otherwise; they do not change the head function. The determinant factor is \\(p^{4-j}\\), giving\n\\[\np^{4-j}p^{(j-2)(4-j)}\n=\np^{(j-1)(4-j)}\n=p^2\n\\]\nfor \\(j=2,3\\). The calculation is independent of the source representation and its reduction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "Lemma 6.4; the open-orbit calculation also supplies Lemma 4.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>At a fixed split finite place away from \\(p\\), deform by the unramified determinant character near \\(\\eta=1\\). Use upper-\\((2,2)\\) pivot tests when the elliptic block and final pair have no norm-power linkage, including the stated potentially good and ramified quadratic Steinberg cases. At the indicated unramified Steinberg places use maximal \\((3,1)\\) parahoric level.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The prescribed tests and their translates span the required fixed tests in theta coinvariants after localization at a Laurent polynomial nonzero at weight zero, with bounded denominators along \\(\\eta\\to1\\). The open rank-two coinvariants are normalized induction of the determinant-twisted source and the trivial binary representation. At a split spherical place this gives\n\\[\n(\\pi\\otimes\\eta)\\oplus|\\cdot|^{-1/2}\\oplus|\\cdot|^{1/2}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Filter Schwartz functions on \\(M_{2,4}\\) by rank. Rank zero would require a character quotient of the generic source, which is impossible. In rank one, fixing the first covector makes the stabilizer Levi \\(\\operatorname{diag}(1,d)\\) require exponent \\(|d|^{-1}\\) in the raw lower Jacquet module: the Weil factor is \\(|d|^2\\) and the orbit Jacobian is \\(|d|\\). In normalized conventions the required exponent is \\(|d|^{-3/2}\\), excluded by temperedness; the supercuspidal Jacquet module is zero.</p><p>These calculations hold over the deformation ring. On compact charts, restriction and orbit integration, or compact-induction coinvariants, show that the difference from each actual Jacquet character becomes invertible after localization at the comparison point. Thus lower-rank coinvariants vanish there.</p><p>The open rank-two orbit gives normalized induction of the determinant-twisted source and trivial binary representation. At weight zero, unitary parabolic products for general linear groups are irreducible. A pivot test is nonzero because its head pairs with a nonzero matrix coefficient; its translates generate. In the Steinberg case, placing the two Steinberg positions on opposite sides of the single-step flag gives nonzero \\((3,1)\\)-parahoric invariants, and a nonzero projection again generates.</p><p>At fixed level only finitely many vectors need spanning. Choose finitely many translates with a spanning minor \\(f\\) satisfying \\(f(1)\\ne0\\); Cramer’s rule gives the expressions after inverting \\(f\\). Since the fixed-place tame character is trivial and \\(\\eta\\to1\\), the denominators are bounded. Tensor the finitely many local expressions. No inverse at the moving prime occurs.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "Lemma 4.5, “Uniform unary moments.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Fix integral position supports, locally constant tests at \\(p\\), their levels, and an ordinary CM test object. Normalize unary jets in the Igusa frames of Proposition 5.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The normalized unary jets are moments of a bounded measure on the product of the integral position lattices. Its norm depends only on the fixed coefficient bounds and \\(p\\)-levels, not on moment degrees or \\(q_i\\).</p><p>Cutting the measure to units gives, at classical degrees, the theta jet with the corresponding unit cut. If every degree tends to infinity and converges on its \\(p\\)-adic branch, the moments converge to the unit moments. On a sufficiently narrow branch, the unit moments, including restored fixed CM unit-scale powers, are analytic in the degrees.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a scalar position test \\(\\varphi\\), let \\(J_{\\mathbf d}\\) be the normalized jet. For a polynomial \\(P(X)=\\sum_{\\mathbf d}a_{\\mathbf d}X^{\\mathbf d}\\) on the compact position lattice \\(\\Lambda\\), set\n\\[\nL_\\varphi(P)=\\sum_{\\mathbf d}a_{\\mathbf d}J_{\\mathbf d},\n\\qquad\n\\|P\\|_\\Lambda=\\sup_{x\\in\\Lambda}|P(x)|_p.\n\\]\nProve\n\\[\n|L_\\varphi(P)|_p\\le C\\|P\\|_\\Lambda.\n\\]\nFor group-algebra-valued tests, apply the scalar argument to each group-basis coefficient before reassembling; do not take square roots inside a growing group algebra.</p><p>Fix the tame Schwartz level, possibly containing \\(q_i\\), and let \\(\\mathcal A\\) be the complete ring of regular functions on the ordinary Hilbert Igusa tower. Canonical differential frames put finite sums of different weights in this one ring. Products of base half-weight thetas are algebraic integral-weight forms after a fixed level enlargement at \\(2\\).</p><p>Products of normalized nearly holomorphic derivatives are rational jet expressions in these base products. They have no horizontal poles: locally a base square is \\(h^2u\\), \\(u\\) invertible; adjoining \\(\\sqrt u\\) is étale, and the half-weight coefficient \\(h\\sqrt u\\) is regular. Projected Gauss–Manin differentiation has regular connection matrix on the torsor of Hodge complements and introduces no inverse of \\(h\\). Cross-products fix signs and give descent. At cusps, logarithmic operators preserve nonnegative orders. Ordinary unit-root projection therefore gives\n\\[\nF_P\\in\\mathcal A[1/p],\n\\qquad\nF_P(\\mathrm{CM})=L_\\varphi(P)^2.\n\\]\nAt CM objects the unit-root and CM splittings agree.</p><p>Choose the position direction to be the distinguished multiplicative CM eigenspace. If the \\(p\\)-test is constant modulo \\(p^s\\), its Fourier transform is supported on \\(p^{-s}\\mathbf Z_p\\), and sufficiently deep lower unipotents fix the test. A fixed stabilizer contains the necessary upper integral and deep lower congruences.</p><p>Use the canonical \\(\\Gamma_0(p^M)\\) section with multiplicative Igusa marking. Compatible Tate-cusp representatives have effective changes in\n\\[\nB(\\mathbf Z_p)K(p^M),\n\\]\nso preserve the position support and coefficient norm without an initial position–momentum interchange. Unit deck transformations of the finite Igusa torsor give a compatible cusp on each component.</p><p>Assume \\(\\|P\\|_\\Lambda\\le1\\). At a compatible cusp, normalized differentiation multiplies the theta expansion by powers of its quadratic index. After the prescribed unit-scale normalization, the polynomial combination has coefficients\n\\[\n\\sum_{Q(x)=\\beta}\\varphi(x)P(x).\n\\]\nThese finite sums have the fixed test bound, and their square has the corresponding convolution bound. Multiorders are treated similarly. Parity projectors are integral because \\(p&gt;2\\). Away from \\(p\\), Fourier normalizations are residue-characteristic powers and roots of unity, hence \\(p\\)-units; moving-prime scalar coefficient tests remain integral.</p><p>After clearing this fixed bound, \\(F_P\\) has integral expansions. If a least positive vertical denominator remained, its reduction would have zero expansion at every compatible cusp, hence vanish on each ordinary component by the expansion principle, contradicting minimality. Thus the initial denominator, even if degree- or tame-level-dependent, is entirely removed.</p><p>Evaluate at CM and take a scalar square root to obtain the norm estimate. Polynomial density extends the functional to continuous functions and gives a bounded measure. Approximation of the unit characteristic function, using the same square and cross-product argument, identifies its unit restriction with the cut test. Off the unit locus, high positive powers tend uniformly to zero. On units,\n\\[\nx^s=\\omega(x)^s\\exp(s\\log\\langle x\\rangle)\n\\]\ngives branchwise analyticity. Fixed coefficient-field unit scales have the same property after narrowing the branch.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.4", "orig": "Lemma 4.6", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the theta data of Proposition 5 and the canonical ordinary cusp charts of Proposition 4.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The mixed Fourier–Jacobi expansions, in algebraic ordinary differential frames and the monomial-dual lattice, have a common bounded denominator, independent of \\(m,i,\\nu_i\\), coefficientwise in the tame group ring. The same holds after every fixed truncated substitution \\(R_b\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>In the mixed realization, the hyperbolic variable is \\(x\\in A\\), with indices \\((x,x)\\). At zero Jacobi argument, positive hyperbolic creations insert position powers without lower Hermite terms. Their differential frames are logarithmic toric frames, so incur no CM periods. The binary columns have the unary normalization of Lemma 5.3. At rational prime-to-\\(p\\) Jacobi torsion, translate by the corresponding rational unipotent and use its Fourier trivialization and prime-to-\\(p\\) splitting of semiabelian differentials. At index zero only the minor using both binary columns survives.</p><p>Choose source integration representatives integral at \\(p\\). The compact quotient gives finite sums with fixed volume denominator. At \\(q_i\\), determinant equivariance cancels the source character, so no growing integration-level denominator occurs. Remaining character values are algebraic \\(p\\)-units.</p><p>Choose boundary representatives integral at \\(p\\), compatible with the multiplicative flag. Translated binary tests remain integrally supported; the Fourier-dualized column is invariant under integral translations, so \\(x\\) is integral. Dense prime-to-\\(p\\) Jacobi torsions can preserve the fixed level. Approximation also permits integrality at moving and fixed exceptional places.</p><p>Expand scalar group-basis coefficient tests into unary tensors retaining the original bounds and supports. Lemma 5.3 bounds the resulting moments; integral position powers and fixed unit scales preserve the bound. The number of summands does not affect a nonarchimedean bound.</p><p>For each fixed weight and index, finitely many algebraic torsion evaluations separate the finite-dimensional algebraic coefficient space. Algebraicity of their values gives algebraicity of the section, initially with a possible individual denominator. The full family of prime-to-\\(p\\) torsion evaluations is dense on the special fiber and detects vertical integrality. The least-denominator argument removes the individual denominator.</p><p>The characteristic-zero expansion principle gives algebraicity of the form, and the ordinary Fourier–Jacobi expansion principle transfers the uniform coefficient bounds to it. The finite étale charts are those of Proposition 4 and require no theta estimate. Finally, binomial substitution through degree \\(b-1\\) has only a fixed factorial loss.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Proposition 5.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the odd-prime contradiction setting, \\(b_*\\), and the theta forms of Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">All measures on compact sources and archimedean tori have the paper’s algebraic normalization; changes to invariant norm formulas cost fixed global scalars.</p><p class=\"li\">Every toric sum at conductor \\(q_i\\) is raw.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Positive weights \\(m_i\\) can be chosen with\n\\[\nm_i\\to+\\infty,\\qquad m_i\\to0\n\\]\non the fixed \\(p\\)-adic branch, so that every constant Fourier–Jacobi coefficient tends coefficientwise to zero modulo \\(t^{b_*}\\). This holds uniformly in bounded algebraic ordinary differential bases over all boundary representatives and polynomial components.</p><p>The weights may satisfy arbitrary additional archimedean lower bounds and further \\(p\\)-adic approximation requirements tending to zero.</p>", "proof": "<p>At index zero the hyperbolic variable vanishes, so only the determinant minor on \\(B\\) remains. Lemma 6.1 identifies every constant coefficient with the scalar \\(S_i(m)\\) times the operators bounded in Lemma 6.4.</p><p>Fix \\(h\\ge2b_*\\). The substitution\n\\[\n[g]\\longmapsto(1+t)^{\\ell_i(g)}\\pmod{t^h}\n\\]\nrespects multiplication modulo \\(p^{n_i-c_h}\\), since the finite group relation maps to \\((1+t)^{p^{n_i}}-1\\) with increasingly divisible coefficients. Bounded group-ring coefficients remain bounded after this fixed truncation.</p><p>If\n\\[\nD(t)=d_0+\\cdots+d_{h-1}t^{h-1}\n\\]\nhas bounded coefficients and \\(v_p(d_0)\\) bounded above, then\n\\[\nD(t)^{-1}\n=\nd_0^{-1}\\sum_{r=0}^{h-1}\n\\bigl(-d_0^{-1}(D(t)-d_0)\\bigr)^r\n\\pmod{t^h}\n\\]\nhas bounded coefficients. Clear factors before truncating; no bounded inverse in the entire group algebra is asserted.</p><p>By Lemma 6.2, \\(P_{0,i}\\to0\\pmod{t^{b_*}}\\), while both periods have bounded higher coefficients. Thus\n\\[\nP_{0,i}^2P_{1,i}^2\\to0\\pmod{t^{2b_*}}.\n\\]\nLemma 6.3 and truncated inversion give \\(S_i(m)^2\\to0\\pmod{t^{2b_*}}\\).</p><p>Write \\(S_i=\\sum_{j&lt;h}s_{ij}t^j\\), with bounded coefficients. First \\(s_{i0}^2\\to0\\), hence \\(s_{i0}\\to0\\). Inductively,\n\\[\n[t^{2j}]S_i^2\n=\ns_{ij}^2+2\\sum_{a&lt;j}s_{ia}s_{i,2j-a}.\n\\]\nThe sum tends to zero by previous steps and boundedness, so \\(s_{ij}\\to0\\) for \\(j&lt;b_*\\). Lemma 6.4 gives uniform vanishing at all cusps.</p><p>For each \\(i\\), choose \\(m\\) sufficiently close to zero to achieve the required period interpolation and clearing-factor bounds. Every sufficiently small congruence class contains arbitrarily large positive integers, permitting all extra lower bounds and approximation requirements. Choose diagonally with precision tending to infinity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "Section 5.1, “The binary scalar and its multiplicity.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the equal-rank binary lift on \\(U(B)=U(A)\\). At split places normalize its intertwiner by\n\\[\nJ_v(\\Phi,z)\n=\n\\int_{\\operatorname{GL}_2(F_v)}\n\\Phi({}^th)|\\det h|_v\n\\pi_v^\\vee(h)\\eta_v^{-1}(\\det h)z\\,d^\\times h,\n\\]\nwith \\(\\operatorname{vol}(\\operatorname{GL}_2(\\mathcal O_{F_v}))=1\\); use spherical-to-spherical maps elsewhere and the determinant highest vector at infinity.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The binary lift has one global scalar \\(S_i(m)\\) multiplying these intertwiners. Include the CM conversion\n\\[\n\\prod_{\\sigma:F\\hookrightarrow\\mathbf R}(2\\pi i/\\Omega_\\sigma)^{2m}\n\\]\nand the fixed unit-scale powers in \\(S_i(m)\\). Its tame group-ring coefficients have a uniform lower valuation bound. Every constant coefficient of the upper lift is \\(S_i(m)\\) times a compact automorphic evaluation and projected local operators.</p>", "proof": "<p>Local theta correspondence gives \\(\\pi\\eta(\\det)\\) at split places; at nonsplit finite places only the spherical line is used, and at infinity the determinant character occurs.</p><p>For multiplicity, let \\(D_{\\mathrm{quat}}/F\\) be ramified exactly at the real places. The image of the central quotient of \\(U(A)\\) in its projective quaternion quotient is\n\\[\n\\omega_{L/F}(\\operatorname{Nrd}(g))=1.\n\\]\nLocal norm obstructions, the Hasse norm theorem, positivity of rational quaternion norms, and quadratic reciprocity give this description.</p><p>Extend functions by zero to the projective quaternion quotient. After Jacquet–Langlands, split good Hecke data determine the prescribed Hilbert representation up to \\(\\omega_{L/F}\\): Galois representations and Chebotarev identify their absolutely irreducible restrictions to \\(G_L\\), so extensions differ by at most this quadratic character; strong multiplicity one concludes. The two twists restrict identically to the indicated part, and restriction is injective since a function supported only on its complement would belong to both distinct twists. Nonsplit hyperspecial factors contribute one spherical line. This gives the required multiplicity one.</p><p>The displayed integral is the central Godement–Jacquet map, by continuation when needed; equivariance, local uniqueness, and a nonzero open test identify it with the theta intertwiner.</p><p>Choose integral open tests at \\(q_i\\) and fixed-level open tests at \\(p\\) carrying a fixed compact vector to itself. At \\(q_i\\) the full source maximal compact has volume one. The binary moment estimate bounds the lift. Evaluate a fixed nonzero algebraic compact automorphic function at a fixed nonzero value and divide by that fixed value. This costs only a fixed denominator and bounds \\(S_i(m)\\). Determinant cancellation prevents a growing source summation level.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "Section 5.3, equation (5.3)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(f_0\\) be the weight-two newform of \\(A_0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P_{0,i}(m)\\) be the raw conductor-\\(q_i\\) toric period over \\(K\\) of its \\((m-1)\\)-st normalized Maass–Shimura raise against \\(\\eta_K^{-1}\\), normalized by integral CM differentials.</p><p class=\"li\">At \\(p\\), use the Kirillov function \\(1_{\\mathbf Z_p^\\times}\\); at \\(q_i\\), use \\(n(1/q_i)\\) and multiply the fixed-measure toric integral by \\(q_i-1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For each \\(i\\), first take \\(m\\) sufficiently close to zero on its branch, and then let \\(i\\) increase. Then\n\\[\nP_{0,i}(m)\\longrightarrow0\\pmod{t^{b_*}},\n\\]\nwith bounded coefficients through every fixed higher truncation.</p>", "proof": "<p>For the cusp parameter \\(u\\), put\n\\[\nf_0^{[p]}(u)=\\sum_{p\\nmid n}a_nu^n,\n\\qquad\nF_0(u)=\\theta^{-1}f_0^{[p]}(u)\n=\n\\sum_{p\\nmid n}\\frac{a_n}{n}u^n.\n\\]\nOn the ordinary Igusa tower,\n\\[\n\\theta^{m-1}f_0^{[p]}\\to F_0\n\\]\nbecause \\(n^{m-1}\\to n^{-1}\\) uniformly on unit indices. The limiting weight is zero, so the expansion principle constructs a global analytic \\(F_0\\) on the completed ordinary good tame curve, descending from the tower, with\n\\[\ndF_0=f_0^{[p]}(u)d\\log u,\n\\qquad\nF_0|_{\\text{compatible cusp}}=0.\n\\]\nThe canonical \\(p\\)-level section and Frobenius maps allow this even for \\(p\\mid N\\).</p><p>Choose \\(\\varphi\\) with \\(\\varphi^*\\omega_0=\\kappa f_0(u)d\\log u\\). For the depletion polynomial \\(E_p(X)=\\sum_re_rX^r\\),\n\\[\ndF_0\n=\n\\kappa^{-1}\\sum_re_rp^{-r}(\\varphi\\circ\\operatorname{Fr}^r)^*\\omega_0.\n\\]\nThe factor \\(p^{-r}\\) accounts for \\(u\\mapsto u^{p^r}\\). These finitely many maps extend to a wide-open thickening.</p><p>The primitive extends to a smaller thickening. Near a missing special-fiber point write\n\\[\nF_0=\\sum_{n\\in\\mathbf Z}a_nz^n.\n\\]\nOverconvergence of \\(dF_0\\) gives exponential decay of negative \\(na_n\\); division by \\(n\\) loses only a subexponential factor. Exactness excludes logarithmic terms. The positive part is bounded. Localization–completion exactness glues these collars without independent constants, giving a connected wide open on each component.</p><p>Use Berkovich–Coleman integration. After finite extension the elliptic target has good or split multiplicative reduction. In the good case the integral is the abelian logarithm. For a Tate curve \\(\\mathbf G_m/Q_0^{\\mathbf Z}\\), choose\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Log}_{Q_0}(z)\n=\n\\operatorname{Log}(z)\n-\n\\frac{v_p(z)}{v_p(Q_0)}\\operatorname{Log}(Q_0),\n\\]\nwhich kills \\(Q_0\\). One branch works for all maps to the same target. Functoriality, the zero cusp constant, and torsion of cusp images give\n\\[\nF_0(x)\n=\n\\kappa^{-1}\\sum_re_rp^{-r}\n\\log_{A_0,p}\\bigl(\\varphi(\\operatorname{Fr}^rx)\\bigr).\n\\]\nThis uses path integration, not equality of derivatives alone.</p><p>At the relevant CM points, Frobenius quotients are horizontal \\(p\\)-translations of conductor-\\(q_i\\) Heegner points, including canonical \\(p\\)-level. Their raw weighted sums are fixed finite combinations of the logarithm sums in Proposition 3. Both tame signs occur there. Differential and character unit powers tend to one as \\(m\\to0\\), proving the assertion without a reduction hypothesis on \\(A_0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.3", "orig": "Lemma 5.2, “Cleared period comparison.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">In addition to \\(P_{0,i}(m)\\), define \\(P_{1,i}(m)\\) from the newform of \\(A_0^{D'}\\), with the same raising, character, raw conductor-\\(q_i\\) normalization, and \\(p\\)-depleted Kirillov test.</p><p class=\"li\">At other places choose fixed nonzero toric tests at weight zero. Both periods have uniformly bounded coefficients.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S_i(m)\\) be the scalar of Lemma 6.1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are group-ring polynomial factors \\(A_i(m),B_i(m)\\), with uniformly bounded coefficients, such that\n\\[\nA_i(m)S_i(m)^2\n=\nB_i(m)P_{0,i}(m)^2P_{1,i}(m)^2.\n\\]\nFor each \\(i\\), after \\(m\\) is sufficiently close to zero, the augmentation of \\(A_i(m)\\) is nonzero with valuation bounded above independently of \\(i\\). The neighborhood may depend on \\(i\\).</p><p>The identity holds in the full characteristic-zero tame group algebra, including at zeros of global central \\(L\\)-values.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use bilinear dual data in the equal-rank Rallis and Waldspurger formulas, so the squares are algebraic squares. The Siegel–Weil input is the anisotropic boundary case \\(m=n=2,r=0,s_0=0\\), where\n\\[\nE(0,\\Phi)=2I(\\Phi).\n\\]\nRestrict to two copies of \\(U(A)\\), pair with source cusp forms, and unfold. Compactness gives convergent theta integrals; the zero-space lift vanishes because it could only be one-dimensional. The toric formulas concern central values, not residues or derivatives.</p><p>Field conjugation is anti-symplectic and identifies the oscillator with its dual by Poisson summation. On the toric side a rational normalizer inducing conjugation converts the second period to the first up to fixed character values. Quadratic base change gives\n\\[\nL(1/2,\\pi_L\\otimes\\eta_L)\n=\nL(1/2,\\pi_{0,K}\\otimes\\eta_K)\nL(1/2,\\pi_{1,K}\\otimes\\eta_K).\n\\]\nFixed adjoint, norm, zeta, and measure terms contribute one fixed scalar.</p><p>At a split local field \\(E\\), write \\(\\chi=\\eta_v^{-1}\\). The matrix integral\n\\[\nZ_G(F,c,\\chi,s)\n=\n\\int_{\\operatorname{GL}_2(E)}\nF(g)c(g)\\chi(\\det g)|\\det g|^{s+1/2}d^\\times g\n\\]\nsatisfies\n\\[\nZ_G(\\widehat F,c^\\vee,\\chi^{-1},1-s)\n=\n\\gamma(s,\\pi\\otimes\\chi,\\psi_{\\mathrm{add}})\nZ_G(F,c,\\chi,s).\n\\]\nAt the center its factor is\n\\[\n\\Gamma_v(\\pi\\otimes\\chi)\n=\n\\epsilon(1/2,\\pi\\otimes\\chi,\\psi_{\\mathrm{add}})\n\\frac{L(1/2,\\pi^\\vee\\otimes\\chi^{-1})}\n     {L(1/2,\\pi\\otimes\\chi)}.\n\\]\nThere is one functional-equation application. Integrating first over invertible matrices factors the Rallis local integral into the binary maps. The additive-to-multiplicative factor\n\\[\n(1-Q^{-1})(1-Q^{-2})\n\\]\ncancels under spherical normalization, leaving no moving denominator \\(1-Q^{-1}\\).</p><p>For\n\\[\nM(W,\\chi,s)=\\int_{E^\\times}W(\\operatorname{diag}(x,1))\n\\chi(x)|x|^{s-1/2}d^\\times x,\n\\]\nand \\(W^w(g)=W(gw)\\), the Whittaker equation gives\n\\[\nM(W^w,\\chi^{-1},1-s)\n=\n\\gamma(s,\\pi\\otimes\\chi,\\psi_{\\mathrm{add}})\nM(W,\\chi,s).\n\\]\nThe identity \\(wa(x^{-1})=x^{-1}a(x)w\\) introduces no extra \\(\\chi(-1)\\). The toric factor is\n\\[\n\\Gamma_v(\\pi\\otimes\\chi)M(W,\\chi,1/2)^2,\n\\]\nwith the same gamma orientation.</p><p>At \\(p\\), \\(\\chi\\) is unramified and the unit Kirillov test gives \\(M=1\\). Gamma, Euler, and epsilon factors cancel, without bounding them individually; \\(v_p(\\chi(p))=m\\) may vary. Continuation applies to every generic elliptic local representation, including special and supercuspidal cases.</p><p>At \\(q=q_i\\), let \\(L_{\\mathrm{fake}}\\) be the unramified degree-two central Euler expression ignoring tame inertia. The translated spherical expansion gives\n\\[\nF_q(\\chi):=(q-1)M(W,\\chi,1/2)\n=\n\\sum_{a\\in\\mathbf F_q^\\times}\n\\chi(a)\\bigl(\\psi_{\\mathrm{add}}(a/q)+L_{\\mathrm{fake}}-1\\bigr).\n\\]\nFor nontrivial tame \\(\\chi\\), this is the Gauss sum. At augmentation,\n\\[\nF_q(1)=-1+(q-1)(L_{\\mathrm{fake}}-1).\n\\]\nPut \\(D_q=L_{\\mathrm{fake}}^{-1}\\). Clearing gives\n\\[\nH_q\n=\nD_q\\sum_a\\chi(a)\\psi_{\\mathrm{add}}(a/q)\n+\n(1-D_q)\\sum_a\\chi(a),\n\\]\n\\[\nH_q(1)=(q-1)-qD_q(1).\n\\]\nBoth have bounded coefficients. The no-eigenvalue-one condition on \\(V(\\gamma)\\) makes \\(D_{q_i}(1)\\), and hence \\(H_{q_i}(1)\\), nonzero with bounded valuation for sufficiently small \\(m\\). Divide only by the full cleared raw factors, never isolated \\(q-1\\), conductor projectors, or individual Gauss sums.</p><p>At infinity, the norm ratio of \\(\\det(Z)^{m-1}\\) to the vacuum is\n\\[\n(m-1)!\\,m!.\n\\]\nThe quaternionic raised weight-two vector has the same ratio. These factorials cancel. Comparing leading position coordinates and normalized Maass–Shimura raising leaves only fixed algebraic \\(p\\)-unit powers. The CM normalization has weight \\(2m\\) per embedding, already included in \\(S_i(m)\\).</p><p>At fixed exceptional places, local factors are rational functions in unramified parameters with fixed inertia. At the tempered center their denominators have no pole and the selected toric functionals are nonzero, so the cost is bounded near weight zero. The remaining fixed global scalar is algebraic by evaluation at a nonzero parameter; if none exists, the theta comparison already vanishes.</p><p>Clear all these factors to obtain the asserted identity. Finite-order characters separate the characteristic-zero group algebra. Cross-multiplication uses the common \\(L\\)-value identity without dividing by a global \\(L\\)-value, so the result holds also at its zeros.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.4", "orig": "Section 5.5, “Projected tests at every cusp.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the projected binary tests at all boundary representatives, with scalar \\(S_i(m)\\) factored as in Lemma 6.1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The remaining compact evaluations and projected local operators have uniform bounds after fixed-truncation inversion of the already controlled clearing factors. The bound is uniform over all cusps, even though their number may grow with \\(q_i\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>At \\(p\\), canonical projection leaves integral matrix support. Expanding the binary integral by determinant valuation gives\n\\[\np^{(m-1)v_p(\\det h)}.\n\\]\nCoset volumes have fixed level, and translates of a fixed compact automorphic function have bounded supremum norm. For large \\(m\\), the series converges \\(p\\)-adically uniformly; its determinant truncations are the Godement–Jacquet series, so continuation gives the same rational operator for arbitrary elliptic local type.</p><p>At \\(q_i\\), the projected test is integral, depends on reduction modulo \\(q_i\\), and retains determinant equivariance. Full rank is bounded with maximal-compact volume one. On deficient rank, the determinant of the stabilizer surjects onto \\(\\Delta_i\\), so its coefficient is a bounded multiple of\n\\[\nN_{\\Delta_i}=\\sum_{\\delta\\in\\Delta_i}[\\delta].\n\\]\nThis follows from integral invariance, with no division by \\(|\\Delta_i|\\).</p><p>For the remaining untwisted integral lattice sum, express exact reduced image conditions by subtracting containment conditions. Each containment sum is a translate and determinant power times \\(L_{\\mathrm{fake}}\\). All volume and translation factors are powers of \\(q_i\\), hence \\(p\\)-units. Thus \\(D_q\\) clears the denominator coefficientwise.</p><p>Other places are spherical or fixed rational no-pole operators. Integral boundary representatives introduce only \\(p\\)-unit frame factors, and source sums retain a fixed volume denominator. The rank-stratum descriptions are uniform over every boundary representative.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Proposition 6.5, “The combined theta output.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Retain the odd-prime contradiction setting and \\(b_*\\). Use the coefficient lattices, fixed-level tests, and permitted replacements of Proposition 5.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are positive integers \\(m_i\\to\\infty\\), tending \\(p\\)-adically to zero on the prescribed branch, and theta forms \\(\\Theta_i\\) of weight\n\\[\n(m_i,m_i,1;-1)\n\\]\nsuch that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Their monomial-dual lattices have one uniform denominator bound, coefficientwise in the tame group algebra and every fixed truncation; one fixed scaling clears it.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Every constant Fourier–Jacobi coefficient tends to zero through degree \\(b_*-1\\), uniformly at all boundary representatives.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. At augmentation, a positive-index coefficient has an integral-frame evaluation of valuation bounded above independently of \\(i\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">4. At the two \\(p\\)-places, \\(p^{-2}U_2,p^{-2}U_3\\) have value one.</p><p class=\"li num\">5. At every controlled upper-\\((2,2)\\) place, including \\(q_i\\), the expanding center is invertible and the final binary data are\n   \\[\n   (|\\cdot|^{-1/2},|\\cdot|^{1/2}).\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">6. At the designated unramified Steinberg places, level is \\((3,1)\\) parahoric without further refinement; nonsplit finite places are hyperspecial.</p><p class=\"li num\">7. At split good spherical places the parameter is\n   \\[\n   (\\pi\\otimes\\mathcal C^{m_i}\\nu_i)\n   \\oplus|\\cdot|^{-1/2}\\oplus|\\cdot|^{1/2}.\n   \\]</p><p>All constants depend only on fixed data, fixed tests, and \\(b_*\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 7.3 gives bounded positive content simultaneously with Proposition 6. Proposition 5 supplies uniform denominators and exact \\(p\\)-operator values.</p><p>Use the bounded fixed-place spanning assertion of Proposition 5 to impose the additional levels. If all resulting controlled forms had content tending to zero, their finitely many fixed prime-to-\\(p\\) translates would also, since such correspondences preserve ordinary integrality up to fixed constants. The bounded spanning expression would force the original form’s content to tend to zero, a contradiction. Fix a surviving choice along the ultrafilter.</p><p>Lemma 7.4 gives the final binary allocations. All chosen tests remain permitted by the theta and constant-term arguments. A fixed-index neat cover and fixed averaging descent, if needed, cost only a fixed constant; no averaging at \\(q_i\\) occurs.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.1, with the nonzero row-one observation following it", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the index represented by \\(x=e_1\\in A\\), with stabilizer \\(T=U(Le_2)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose a fixed tensor vector \\(\\varphi\\in\\pi^\\vee\\) and finite-order character \\(d_0\\) of \\([T]\\), unramified at nonsplit places, such that\n  \\[\n  I_T(\\varphi,d_0)=\\int_{[T]}\\varphi(s)d_0(s)\\,ds\\ne0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(d_1,d_2\\ge0\\), \\(d_1+d_2=m-1\\), and use the monomial\n  \\[\n  [e,B_1]^{d_1}[e,B_2]^{d_2}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Permitted finite tests make this positive coefficient unfold as a fixed row-one Heisenberg theta function times\n\\[\n\\int_{[T]}\n\\varphi(s)\\eta^{-1}(s)\n\\vartheta_{1,d_1}(sb_1)\\vartheta_{2,d_2}(sb_2)\\,ds.\n\\]\nAt \\(q_i\\), the only extra row-two condition is a unit cut in the first binary column. The factor is a fixed nonzero volume, with no \\(q_i-1\\). The row-one factor can be evaluated nontrivially at a fixed finite collection of prime-to-\\(p\\) Jacobi torsions.</p>", "proof": "<p>A nonzero compact vector spherical at nonsplit places can be translated at split places to be nonzero on \\(T\\): nonsplit determinants are realized by maximal compacts, and strong approximation on \\(SU(A)\\) away from a split place removes the remaining components. Fourier expansion of its nonzero restriction to the compact torus quotient supplies \\(d_0\\). A fixed diagonal translation at \\(p\\) makes \\(\\varphi_p\\) invariant under the integral lower unipotent without destroying the period.</p><p>At a fixed split place, choose small support transverse to the norm-one orbit. At nonsplit places, primitive norm-one vectors form one compact orbit. Both cuts reduce integration to \\(T\\).</p><p>At \\(q_i\\), integral vector–covector pairs pairing to one form one \\(\\operatorname{GL}_2(\\mathcal O_q)\\)-orbit. At augmentation the pivot determinant character is trivial. Setting the position to \\(e_1\\) makes head invertibility the unit condition on the second entry of \\(Y_{B_1}\\). The orbit volume is the original compact-to-torus ratio, not a residue-unit-group order.</p><p>At \\(p\\), choose \\(Y_e\\) close to \\(e_1\\), any prescribed fixed locally constant unit test on the second entry of \\(Y_{B_1}\\), and integral remaining columns. A fixed deep source subgroup enlarged by the lower unipotent gives the orbit cut; that unipotent fixes \\(\\varphi_p\\) and preserves the relevant row-two entry. The tests retain the required pivot form.</p><p>At infinity, rotating \\(x\\) contributes the determinant already in the source type. The minors become the second-row powers. Heisenberg translation acts on row one, giving the asserted factorization.</p><p>The row-one function is nonzero by its Fourier realization. Finite-adelic approximation gives a rational position with nonzero finite test, the real Gaussian is nonzero, and modulation separates positions. Dense prime-to-\\(p\\) torsion arguments detect it. Move the finitely many chosen torsions to zero with compatible unipotents and frames. Only a fixed finite list of nonzero constants and denominators is introduced.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "Lemma 6.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Split the \\(T\\)-action into two line oscillators with inverse splitting characters, each restricting to \\(\\omega_{L/F}\\), with opposite infinity exponents \\(\\pm1/2\\) in unitary normalization.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose characters \\(\\xi_j\\) of parallel weights \\(d_j+c_j\\), where \\(c_1+c_2=1\\), with\n  \\[\n  \\xi_1\\xi_2=\\mathcal C^m d_0,\n  \\]\n  unramified at nonsplit places and with \\(\\xi_2\\) unramified at \\(p\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define the unit-cut unary periods\n  \\[\n  T_j(d_j)\n  =\n  \\int_{[U(1)]}\\vartheta_{j,d_j}(s)\\xi_j(s)^{-1}\\,ds.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The fixed finite-order parts of \\(\\xi_1,\\xi_2\\) can be chosen, preserving their product, with permitted local tests such that\n\\[\nT_1(d_{10})\\ne0,\\qquad T_2(d_{20})\\ne0\n\\]\nat positive classical cut weights. The weights can satisfy that \\(d_{10}+d_{20}+1\\) is divisible by every prescribed torsion order and a prescribed sufficiently large fixed power of \\(p\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose large weights with these congruences. The line theta Hom is nonzero locally: at nonsplit finite places use the unramified matched lattice correspondence, at infinity the chosen Fock degree, and at split places there is no obstruction.</p><p>For each line separately, its standard character is\n\\[\n\\chi_W^{-1}\\alpha_L,\\qquad \\alpha_L(z)=\\alpha(z/z^c).\n\\]\nIt restricts to \\(\\omega_{L/F}\\) on \\(\\mathbf A_F^\\times\\). Dimension-one epsilon dichotomy identifies each local epsilon factor with the product of the local line invariants. Coherence and the product formula make each global root number \\(+1\\), not merely their product.</p><p>After the arithmetic norm-half shift, and conjugating CM orientation if necessary, its critical algebraic type is\n\\[\n(a_j+1)\\Sigma-a_j\\Sigma^c,\\qquad a_j=d_j+O(1)\\ge0,\n\\]\nwith split conductor. If \\(u_j\\) is the unitary standard character, put\n\\[\n\\lambda_j=u_j|\\cdot|_L^{-1/2}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\lambda_j|_{\\mathbf A_F^\\times}\n=\n\\omega_{L/F}|\\cdot|_F^{-1},\n\\qquad\nL(1,\\lambda_j\\nu)=L(1/2,u_j\\nu).\n\\]</p><p>Apply the characteristic-zero nonvanishing theorem of Burungale–He–Tian–Ye [17] at a degree-one split prime \\(\\mathfrak l\\) above an odd \\(\\ell\\), away from all fixed data and \\(p\\). The maximal \\(\\mathbf Z_\\ell\\)-free anticyclotomic extension unramified outside \\(\\mathfrak l\\) has group \\(\\mathbf Z_\\ell\\). Both signs remain \\(+1\\) for sufficiently ramified characters. Avoid the finite exceptional sets for\n\\[\nL(1/2,u_1\\nu),\\qquad L(1/2,u_2\\nu^{-1}).\n\\]</p><p>An \\(\\ell\\)-power-order \\(\\nu\\) has a unique square root. Restrict \\(\\nu^{1/2}\\) to \\(U(1)\\), obtaining \\(\\tau\\) with \\(\\tau(z/z^c)=\\nu(z)\\). Replace \\(\\xi_1,\\xi_2\\) by \\(\\xi_1\\tau,\\xi_2\\tau^{-1}\\). Their product is preserved and conductor is added only at the fixed split prime.</p><p>The global line-lift criterion gives nonzero unary periods with suitable local tests. At \\(p\\), use the permitted unit-character test in the first column and the unit cut of the unramified second test; their Mellin integrals are nonzero. Keep nonsplit spherical tests. The output is a character and is nonzero at identity. Fix all choices permanently and enlarge \\(S\\) before realizing the final prime sequence.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.3", "orig": "Lemma 6.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the unary periods and fixed tests of Lemmas 7.1 and 7.2, initially before the additional fixed-place controls.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are positive \\(d_{1i},d_{2i}\\), both tending ordinarily to infinity, for which\n\\[\nm_i=d_{1i}+d_{2i}+1\\to0\n\\]\n\\(p\\)-adically, and a positive-index theta coefficient has augmentation valuation bounded above. The choices can satisfy the constant-term precision of Proposition 6.</p>", "proof": "<p>The moment result in Proposition 5 gives analytic limiting unit periods \\(T_j(s)\\) on fixed narrow discs. Their nonzero cut values from Lemma 7.2 show neither is identically zero. The congruence on the sum makes the discs compatible under \\(s\\mapsto-1-s\\). Choose \\(a\\) avoiding the zero sets of\n\\[\nT_1(s),\\qquad T_2(-1-s).\n\\]\nChoose positive integer sequences \\(d_{1i}\\to a\\), \\(d_{2i}\\to-1-a\\), on the torsion branches and tending ordinarily to infinity. Their period product stays bounded away from zero.</p><p>Restore the unit cut above \\(q_i\\). Subtracting a uniformizer translate from the integer unary test and using torus equivariance produces finitely many factors\n\\[\n1-\\alpha_{iv}\\mathcal C(\\operatorname{Fr}_{iv})^{\\pm d_1}.\n\\]\nThe \\(\\alpha_{iv}\\) have nonzero \\(p\\)-unit limits. After sublimits for bounded-degree roots, these are nonzero analytic functions, because\n\\[\n\\mathcal C(\\operatorname{Fr}_{iv})\\to\\mathcal C(\\gamma)\n\\]\nand \\(\\mathcal C(\\gamma)\\) is a non-torsion \\(p\\)-unit with nonzero unit logarithm. Refine \\(a\\) to avoid these zeros.</p><p>Averaging the binary translates against \\(\\xi_j^{-1}\\) separates the coefficient into\n\\[\nI_T(\\varphi,d_0)T_1(d_1)T_2(d_2)\n\\]\ntimes the nonzero row-one and local factors. These averages use only a fixed finite set of torus representatives: auxiliary characters and torus levels are fixed, and the moving unit tests are invariant under local torus units. Hence at least one of finitely many coefficient evaluations has valuation bounded above; the ultrafilter fixes an option.</p><p>At each stage the positive integers can be arbitrarily close to their limits and arbitrarily large. Their sum plus one can therefore meet every fixed-\\(i\\) constant-term precision required by Proposition 6.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.4", "orig": "Section 6.5, “Positive Hecke controls on the final binary block.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>At an upper-\\((2,2)\\) level, retain the strictly expanding center operator and the degree-one spherical operator of the final \\(\\operatorname{GL}_2\\) in normalized Jacquet restriction.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The prescribed tests have the final allocation\n\\[\n(|\\cdot|^{-1/2},|\\cdot|^{1/2}),\n\\]\nand invertible center expansion. The positive controls commute and have normalizations involving only residue-characteristic powers.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lift final-block expanding double cosets with the upper shifts. The invertible head determines exactly one surviving upper shift for each last-block coset. For both \\(\\varpi^{-1}1_2\\) and \\(\\operatorname{diag}(\\varpi^{-1},1)\\), the determinant factor in the split Weil action cancels the Jacquet half-modulus. The integral trailing columns therefore carry the spherical action of the trivial binary representation, with Satake pair\n\\[\n(Q^{-1/2},Q^{1/2}).\n\\]\nA principal congruence level in the head does not change the count. Iwahori decomposition gives commutativity, and inversion of the contracting operator identifies the controls with the Jacquet operators by Jacquet lifting.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Proposition 7.6, “Classical cusp congruence.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the theta forms of Proposition 7, scaled once to be integral.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(b=b_*\\), \\(\\mathcal O_b=\\mathcal O[t]/(t^b)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose \\(M_i\\to\\infty\\) slowly enough that all constant terms vanish modulo \\(p^{M_i}\\) through degree \\(b-1\\), and\n  \\[\n  M_i\\le n_i-c_b.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H_i\\) be sufficiently large, divisible by \\((p-1)p^{M_i+c}\\), and set\n  \\[\n  \\lambda_i'=(m_i+H_i,m_i+H_i,1+H_i;-1-H_i)\n  \\]\n  at both real places.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal S_i\\) be the corresponding genuine classical cusp space at the prescribed levels, with the lattice of sections integral on the canonical ordinary branch.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(T_i^0\\) be the commuting integral operator algebra generated by split good spherical coefficients in \\(Q_v^{3/2}\\)-normalization, the normalized \\(p\\)-operators, and the prescribed positive binary controls.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After decreasing \\(M_i\\) by a fixed constant, there are \\(\\mathcal O\\)-algebra maps\n\\[\n\\lambda_i^0:T_i^0\\longrightarrow\n(\\mathcal O/p^{M_i}\\mathcal O)[t]/(t^{b_*}),\n\\qquad M_i\\to\\infty,\n\\]\nsending all generators to their theta comparison values in the full truncated ring.</p><p>The two normalized \\(p\\)-operators have value one. The map factors through the local summand determined by the residual augmentation system, where those operators are units. No reduction of \\(T_i^0\\) is asserted.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 8.1 gives a lifted cusp section \\(s_i\\). The ordinary-integral classical lattice is full and bounded: finitely many ordinary evaluations bound it above, and clearing denominators of a basis bounds it below. If \\(Q_i^0\\) denotes ordinary integral sections, its definition\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_i\\cap Q_i^0\n\\]\nshows that ordinary divisibility gives divisibility in this lattice without extra loss. Lemma 8.2 makes the specified operators integral. Their algebra \\(T_i^0\\) is a finite torsion-free \\(\\mathcal O\\)-module inside its endomorphism lattice.</p><p>All comparison values lie in fixed \\(k\\) and are integral. The bound \\(M_i\\le n_i-c_b\\) makes tame relations valid in the full ring. Evaluate the polynomial algebra on the Hecke generators at these values.</p><p>For any integral polynomial relation \\(P\\) acting as zero on \\(\\mathcal S_i\\), the ordinary congruences and integral operators give\n\\[\nP(\\text{comparison values})\\,s_i\\equiv0\\pmod{p^{M_i}}.\n\\]\nThere is no precision loss depending on polynomial degree or number of terms.</p><p>Evaluate the positive coefficient retained in Proposition 7. Its value \\(a_i(t)\\) is integral and\n\\[\nv_p(a_i(0))\\le C\n\\]\nuniformly; the literal dual pairing and Hasse shift preserve this. Lemma 8.3 gives\n\\[\nP(\\text{comparison values})\\equiv0\n\\pmod{p^{M_i-bC},t^b}.\n\\]\nDescend divisibility from the finite evaluation field using\n\\[\np^n\\mathcal O_{K'}\\cap\\mathcal O=p^n\\mathcal O.\n\\]\nThe loss is fixed for all relations, so evaluation factors through \\(T_i^0\\).</p><p>A finite algebra over complete local \\(\\mathcal O\\) decomposes into residual local factors. The target is local, so the map selects the residual augmentation factor. Both normalized \\(p\\)-operators map to one and are units there.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "Section 7.3, “Hasse extension and the shifted weight.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the ordinary cusp section modulo \\((p^{M_i},t^b)\\) furnished by Propositions 4 and 7.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After a sufficiently large divisible Hasse shift \\(H_i\\), the section lifts coefficientwise to a characteristic-zero cusp section of weight \\(\\lambda_i'\\), retaining its ordinary coefficients modulo the working precision.</p>", "proof": "<p>The ordinary open is the nonvanishing locus of total Hasse. Localization on a finite affine cover extends a coherent-sheaf section after multiplication by a sufficiently high Hasse power, simultaneously for all \\(t\\)-coefficients.</p><p>A sufficiently large \\(p\\)-power of Hasse lifts modulo \\(p^M\\): local lifts differ by \\(p\\) on overlaps, and their \\(p^r\\)-th powers agree modulo \\(p^{r+1}\\), with transition functions included. In multiplicative frames this lift is one modulo \\(p^M\\), and its exponent can be increased arbitrarily.</p><p>Twist farther by the ample Hodge line. Serre vanishing gives surjective reduction on global sections, yielding a characteristic-zero lift in the same Artin coefficient ring. The source sheaf is twisted by minus the pulled-back boundary, so the lift is genuinely cuspidal. The shift changes only the two line factors and gives \\(\\lambda_i'\\). Its size and the cusp-space dimension may vary with \\(i\\), without loss of precision. Fixed neat descent costs only a fixed valuation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.2", "orig": "Lemma 7.4, “Integral trace calculation.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>For the original or shifted weight define\n\\[\n\\widetilde U_j=p^{-N_j(\\lambda')}U_j,\n\\qquad\nN_2=2+\\lambda_3'+\\lambda_4',\n\\quad\nN_3=3+\\lambda_4'.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>These operators preserve the ordinary monomial-dual lattice and all its reductions. After Hasse shift, their comparison values remain one modulo the prescribed precision.</p>", "proof": "<p>At one distinguished factor, Serre–Tate coordinates give\n\\[\nR=\\mathcal O[[q_1-1,q_2-1,q_3-1]].\n\\]\nFor \\(U_j\\), the expanded multiplicative directions are selected by \\(p^{j(3-j)}\\) graphs. For each graph, the remaining extension choices adjoin \\(p\\)-th roots of the \\(j\\) retained coordinates:\n\\[\nR'=R[u_1,\\ldots,u_j]/(u_a^p-q_a).\n\\]\nThe monomial basis with exponents \\(0,\\ldots,p-1\\) has trace zero except at \\(1\\), whose trace is \\(p^j\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}_{R'/R}(R')\\subseteq p^jR.\n\\]\nThe same calculation applies in boundary cones. Total coset counts are \\(p^{j(4-j)}\\).</p><p>Flags and differential maps depend only on the graph and can be taken outside trace. Pullback scales \\(3-j\\) positive differential directions by \\(p\\), and the opposite line by \\(p^{-1}\\). For \\(j=2\\), the positive map is \\(\\operatorname{diag}(1,1,p)\\), so the minor-dual basis scales by \\(p^{a_{13}+a_{23}}\\), integrally and without factorial. Its determinant contributes \\(p^{\\lambda_3'}\\); the opposite line contributes \\(p^{\\lambda_4'}\\). For \\(j=3\\), the positive map is the identity and only the opposite factor occurs. The lower bounds before trace are therefore\n\\[\n\\lambda_3'+\\lambda_4'=N_2-2,\n\\qquad\n\\lambda_4'=N_3-3.\n\\]\nTrace supplies the remaining divisibility.</p><p>For the original weight, \\(N_2=N_3=2\\), so theta values are one. The shift \\((H,H,H;-H)\\) changes \\(N_2\\) by zero and \\(N_3\\) by \\(-H\\), exactly matching differential scaling. Divisible \\(H\\) kills remaining unit-frame powers modulo \\(p^M\\), and Hasse is one in canonical frames. Prime-to-\\(p\\) correspondences similarly preserve the branch and lattice and their additional unit powers become one.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.3", "orig": "Lemma 7.5, “Uniform cancellation in a truncated ring.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(K'/k\\) be finite and\n\\[\na(t)\\in\\mathcal O_{K'}[t]/(t^b),\n\\qquad v_p(a(0))\\le C,\n\\]\nwhere \\(C\\ge0\\) is fixed.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If\n\\[\na(t)r(t)\\in p^M\\mathcal O_{K'}[t]/(t^b),\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\nr(t)\\in p^{M-bC}\\mathcal O_{K'}[t]/(t^b).\n\\]\nThe bound is independent of \\(K'\\) and \\(a\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(a=a_0(1+u)\\), \\(u\\in tK'[t]/(t^b)\\). Then\n\\[\na^{-1}=a_0^{-1}\\sum_{j=0}^{b-1}(-u)^j\n\\in p^{-bC}\\mathcal O_{K'}[t]/(t^b).\n\\]\nMultiply by this inverse.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Proposition 8.3, “Simultaneous transfer.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\pi\\) be a cuspidal automorphic representation occurring in Proposition 8.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G=U(B\\oplus\\mathbb H)\\); it is \\(U(3,1)\\) at both real places and quasi-split at finite places.</p><p class=\"li\">Its infinite component is the holomorphic discrete series of lowest compact type \\(\\lambda'\\); nonsplit finite components are hyperspecial spherical.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose \\(H\\ge3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a unitary cuspidal \\(\\tau\\) on \\(\\operatorname{GL}_d(\\mathbf A_L)\\), write \\(\\tau[a]\\) for its Arthur block and\n  \\[\n  S_a=\\{(a-1)/2,(a-3)/2,\\ldots,-(a-1)/2\\}.\n  \\]\n  Its local parameter is\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{rec}_w(\\tau_w[a])\n  =\n  \\bigoplus_{s\\in S_a}\\operatorname{rec}_w(\\tau_w)|\\cdot|_w^s,\n  \\]\n  with direct-sum monodromy, not a new Steinberg block.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is one formal parameter\n\\[\n\\Psi=\\boxplus_\\beta\\tau_\\beta[a_\\beta],\n\\qquad\n\\sum_\\beta d_\\beta a_\\beta=4,\n\\]\nwith unitary cuspidal factors, such that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Its full archimedean infinitesimal character is the standard transfer of that of \\(\\pi\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. At every split finite \\(v=ww^c\\), \\(\\pi_v\\), viewed as a representation of \\(\\operatorname{GL}_4(F_v)\\), is attached to \\(\\Psi_w\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. At every nonsplit finite place, the standard parameter is unramified with zero monodromy.</p><p>Each cuspidal factor is conjugate-self-dual, tempered at infinity, and regular algebraic after the parity-correcting twist described below. The formal parameter is elliptic.</p>", "proof": "<p>Take the single parameter of Lemma 9.1 and apply Lemma 9.2. The matching at split places already concerns that same parameter, including \\(p\\), bad places, and moving levels. The nonsplit direct-sum calculation gives unramifiedness and zero monodromy. No restriction on the coefficient prime is introduced.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "Lemma 8.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Retain \\(\\pi,\\lambda'\\) of Proposition 9. Its Harish–Chandra coordinates are\n\\[\n\\mathbf k=\n(m+H+\\tfrac12,\\ m+H-\\tfrac12,\\ H-\\tfrac12,\\ \\tfrac12-H).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Labesse’s base-change theorem applies with the sign property for the individual holomorphic discrete series at both real places. It supplies a single conjugate-dual formal parameter with the full infinite character and matching at every split finite and every unramified place. Every cuspidal factor is unramified at nonsplit finite places.</p>", "proof": "<p>The successive gaps are \\(1,m,2H-1\\), so the character is regular integral. The chosen holomorphic series \\(D\\) has negative-sign coordinate \\(k_4\\). Choose its individual pseudocoefficient with trace one on \\(D\\) and zero on all other basic standard modules; in particular it kills properly induced standard modules.</p><p>A unitary representation with this regular integral character is an admissible cohomologically induced \\(A_{\\mathfrak q}(\\mu)\\). Write the ordered theta-stable Levi as \\(\\prod_jU(p_j,q_j)\\), with \\(\\sum p_j=3,\\sum q_j=1\\). Coordinates in each block form a consecutive string with difference one. If the Levi is noncompact, the block containing the negative coordinate has size at least two. In its standard-module resolution, discrete-series terms reorder only within Levi blocks. For \\(D\\) to occur, the negative block would contain \\(k_4\\), hence also \\(k_3\\), requiring \\(k_3-k_4=1\\), contrary to \\(2H-1\\ge5\\). Thus its pseudocoefficient trace is zero. Compact Levis give discrete series, and only \\(D\\) has trace one.</p><p>The product over the real places satisfies Labesse’s sign property, including competitors of different real types. Labesse’s Corollary 5.3 gives one transfer induced from conjugate-dual discrete general-linear representations; the Mœglin–Waldspurger classification writes these as Arthur blocks. The same theorem retains the full infinitesimal character and matches every split finite place and every unramified place. Its simple trace-formula argument applies since \\([F:\\mathbf Q]=2\\), without the weighted fundamental lemma.</p><p>At nonsplit finite places the transfer is spherical, so its parameter has trivial inertia and zero monodromy. The direct-sum Arthur-block formula forces the same for every shifted cuspidal summand. Hence every factor is unramified there.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.2", "orig": "Lemma 8.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the parameter of Lemma 9.1. The full first infinitesimal coordinates are\n\\[\n(k_1,k_2,k_3,k_4)\n=\n(\\lambda_1'+\\tfrac12,\\lambda_2'-\\tfrac12,\n \\lambda_3'-\\tfrac32,\\lambda_4'+\\tfrac32).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Each cuspidal factor \\(\\tau\\) is conjugate-self-dual and tempered at infinity. Choose a unitary character \\(\\chi\\) of \\(L\\) with\n\\[\n\\chi^c=\\chi^{-1},\n\\qquad\n\\chi|_{\\mathbf A_F^\\times}=\\omega_{L/F},\n\\]\ninfinity type \\(z/|z|\\) at the distinguished embeddings, and split auxiliary conductor. For a factor of rank \\(d\\) and length \\(a\\), choose\n\\[\n\\delta\\in\\{0,1\\},\n\\qquad\n\\delta\\equiv a+d-1\\pmod2.\n\\]\nThen \\(\\Pi=\\tau\\chi^\\delta\\) is regular algebraic conjugate-self-dual. Blocks occur without repetition.</p>", "proof": "<p>For an infinity character \\(z^u\\bar z^v\\) in \\(\\tau\\), the shifted coordinates \\(u+s,v+s\\) occur in the prescribed characters. Thus \\(u,v\\) are real and \\(u+v\\in\\mathbf Z\\). Unitary genericity gives \\(|\\operatorname{Re}(u+v)|&lt;1\\), so \\(u+v=0\\). This proves temperedness.</p><p>Then \\(\\tau\\) and \\(\\tau^{c,\\vee}\\) have identical infinity parameters. A distinct conjugate-dual pair, or a repeated identical block, would repeat coordinates in the regular full character. Thus the factors are conjugate-self-dual and the parameter elliptic.</p><p>Construct \\(\\chi\\) by the ray-class argument with prescribed restriction, infinity type, and unramified nonsplit components; a sufficiently deep split conductor removes the unit obstruction. Since\n\\[\n\\chi(x)\\chi(x^c)=\\omega_{L/F}(N_{L/F}x)=1,\n\\]\nit has the required conjugate inverse.</p><p>For a first coordinate \\(u\\) of \\(\\tau\\),\n\\[\nu\\in\\mathbf Z+(4-a)/2,\n\\]\nso\n\\[\nu+\\delta/2\\in\\mathbf Z+(d-1)/2.\n\\]\nThis is the cohomological algebraicity parity. Distinctness follows from the full coordinates; conjugate self-duality follows from that of \\(\\tau\\) and \\(\\chi\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Proposition 8.5. External input: Theorem 8.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\pi\\) be a characteristic-zero cusp eigensystem of Proposition 8, with parameter from Proposition 9.</p><p class=\"li\">Use geometric reciprocity and call the de Rham weight of \\(\\epsilon^{-1}\\) equal to one.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the external regular algebraic conjugate-self-dual Galois theorem stated as Theorem 8.4: for regular algebraic conjugate-self-dual cuspidal \\(\\Pi\\) on \\(\\operatorname{GL}_d(\\mathbf A_L)\\), there is semisimple \\(r_p(\\Pi)\\) with\n  \\[\n  r_p(\\Pi)^c\\simeq r_p(\\Pi)^\\vee\\epsilon^{1-d},\n  \\]\n  de Rham weights \\((d-1)/2-b_j\\) for first infinitesimal coordinates \\(b_j\\), and\n  \\[\n  \\iota\\operatorname{WD}(r_p(\\Pi)|_{G_{L_w}})^{F\\text{-ss}}\n  \\preceq\n  \\operatorname{rec}_w(\\Pi_w|\\det|_w^{(1-d)/2}).\n  \\]\n  Here \\(\\preceq\\) means equal semisimplified Weil representations and dominated monodromy partitions. Spherical places are unramified away from \\(p\\) and crystalline above \\(p\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a continuous semisimple\n\\[\n\\rho_\\pi:G_L\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{GL}_4(\\overline{\\mathbf Q}_p),\n\\qquad\n\\rho_\\pi^c\\simeq\\rho_\\pi^\\vee\\epsilon^{-3},\n\\]\nunramified at all nonsplit finite places, with\n\\[\n\\iota\\operatorname{WD}(\\rho_\\pi|_{G_{L_w}})^{F\\text{-ss}}\n\\preceq\n\\operatorname{rec}_w(\\pi_v|\\det|_w^{-3/2})\n\\]\nat every split \\(v=ww^c\\). It is de Rham above \\(p\\), with increasing distinguished weights\n\\[\n(h_1,h_2,h_3,h_4)\n=\n(1-\\lambda_1',\\,2-\\lambda_2',\\,3-\\lambda_3',\\,-\\lambda_4').\n\\]\nFor \\(\\lambda'=(m+H,m+H,1+H;-1-H)\\), these are\n\\[\n1-m-H,\\quad2-m-H,\\quad2-H,\\quad1+H,\n\\]\nwith gaps \\(h_3-h_2=m\\) and \\(h_4-h_3=2H-1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For each factor \\(\\tau\\) of rank \\(d\\), length \\(a\\), and parity \\(\\delta\\), put\n\\[\n\\Pi=\\tau\\chi^\\delta,\\qquad\nt_s=s+(d-4)/2,\\qquad\n\\eta_s=\\chi^{-\\delta}|\\cdot|_L^{t_s}.\n\\]\nThe infinity exponents of \\(\\eta_s\\) are \\(t_s-\\delta/2,t_s+\\delta/2\\), both integers because\n\\[\n2t_s\\equiv a+d-5\\equiv\\delta\\pmod2.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\eta_s\\) is algebraic. Define\n\\[\n\\rho_\\pi\n=\n\\bigoplus_\\beta\\ \\bigoplus_{s\\in S_{a_\\beta}}\nr_p(\\Pi_\\beta)\\otimes r_p(\\eta_{\\beta,s}).\n\\]\nIts dimension is four. Its local normalization is\n\\[\n(\\tau\\chi^\\delta)|\\det|^{(1-d)/2}\\eta_s\n=\n\\tau|\\det|^{s-3/2},\n\\]\nso the external compatibility and the Arthur formula give the asserted local bound. At nonsplit places, trivial semisimple inertia and zero allowed monodromy force actual unramifiedness. De Rhamness is preserved.</p><p>Since\n\\[\n\\eta_s^c\\eta_{-s}=|\\cdot|_L^{d-4}\n\\]\nand geometric reciprocity sends \\(|\\cdot|_L\\) to \\(\\epsilon\\), opposite shifted summands pair with twist \\(\\epsilon^{-3}\\), proving polarization.</p><p>For a coordinate \\(u\\) of \\(\\tau\\), with \\(k=u+s\\), the de Rham weight is\n\\[\n\\frac{d-1}{2}-u-\\frac\\delta2-\n\\left(t_s-\\frac\\delta2\\right)\n=\n\\frac32-k.\n\\]\nSubstitute the full coordinates from Proposition 9.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Lemmas 8.6, 8.7, and 8.8", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work on the residual local operator summands of Proposition 8, where \\(\\widetilde U_2,\\widetilde U_3\\) are units.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the representations \\(\\rho_\\pi\\) of Proposition 10.</p><p class=\"li\">At a split upper-\\((2,2)\\) control place \\(v\\), let \\(A_v(Z)\\) be the monic Frobenius polynomial of the final binary allocation, obtained from its determinant and degree-one control.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P_v(Z)\\) be the degree-four Frobenius polynomial in the integral good operator algebra, obtained by simultaneous Chebotarev approximation. Divide it monically by \\(A_v\\), writing \\(C_v\\) for the quadratic quotient.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\Delta_v=\n  \\prod_{d=-4}^{4}\n  \\operatorname{Res}_Z\\bigl(A_v(Z),Q_v^{2d}C_v(Q_v^{-d}Z)\\bigr),\n  \\qquad\n  \\Delta=\\prod_v\\Delta_v.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The local flags, separated binary inertia identities, and Steinberg inertia bounds are those of Lemmas 11.1–11.3. In particular the augmentation of \\(\\Delta\\) is nonzero with uniformly bounded valuation.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Lemmas 11.1–11.3 at the stipulated local levels. The degree-four local characteristic coefficients lie in the good integral operator algebra because simultaneous good Frobenius approximations converge in its finite closed integral lattice. Monic division gives \\(A_vC_v=P_v\\) on every characteristic-zero system. Thus all local identities and the separation bound concern the same operator orders and Galois representations.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.1", "orig": "Lemma 8.6, “The two Hodge–Newton contacts.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Retain Proposition 11 and the weights \\(h_j\\) of Proposition 10.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At distinguished \\(w\\mid p\\), \\(\\rho_\\pi\\) has a stable flag of dimensions \\(2,3,4\\), with last quotient\n\\[\nX_w=\\epsilon^{-h_4}\\operatorname{unr}(\\widetilde U_3^{-1}).\n\\]\nAt \\(w^c\\), it has a first line\n\\[\nY_{w^c}=X_w^{-c}\\epsilon^{-3}.\n\\]\nUnder the full-ring congruence, these tend uniformly on all local sequences, coefficientwise in \\(t\\), to\n\\[\nx=\\epsilon^{-1},\\qquad y=\\epsilon^{-2}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Finite-slope Jacquet restriction allocates unramified final roots \\(\\alpha_3,\\alpha_4\\), with normalization\n\\[\n\\widetilde U_2=\\frac{p^{h_3+h_4}}{\\alpha_3\\alpha_4},\n\\qquad\n\\widetilde U_3=\\frac{p^{h_4}}{\\alpha_4}.\n\\]\nTheir unit values give slopes \\(h_3,h_4\\). The remaining slopes sum to \\(h_1+h_2\\). Weak admissibility places Newton above Hodge, so the lowest two sum exactly to \\(h_1+h_2\\); similarly the lowest three sum to \\(h_1+h_2+h_3\\). The strict Hodge gaps force strict Newton gaps.</p><p>After a semistable extension, the canonical low-slope spaces are inertia- and monodromy-stable. Their induced Hodge numbers are at least the sums of the lowest Hodge weights and at most their equal Newton numbers; hence they are weakly admissible. The Colmez–Fontaine subobject correspondence gives subrepresentations, descending because the slope spaces are canonical. The final quotient is the stated crystalline character, and polarization gives the conjugate line.</p><p>Here \\(h_4=1+H_i\\) and \\(H_i\\) is divisible by \\((p-1)p^{M_i+c}\\), so\n\\[\nu^{H_i}\\equiv1\\pmod{p^{M_i}}\n\\quad(u\\in\\mathbf Z_p^\\times)\n\\]\nuniformly. Continuous powers of the unit \\(\\widetilde U_3^{-1}\\) give the unramified factor, whose full-ring image is one. Thus the stated congruences hold on every sequence of local elements.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.2", "orig": "Lemma 8.7, “Separation and inertia.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use \\(\\Delta\\), \\(A_v\\), and \\(C_v\\) of Proposition 11.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The augmentation values of \\(\\Delta\\) are nonzero with uniformly bounded valuation. On every characteristic-zero system with \\(\\Delta\\ne0\\), the Frobenius primary projection \\(P_v^{\\mathrm{bin}}\\) onto the roots of \\(A_v\\) satisfies\n\\[\n(\\rho_\\pi(\\iota)-1)P_v^{\\mathrm{bin}}=0\n\\qquad(\\iota\\in I_{L_v}).\n\\]\nThis is an actual inertia identity, allowing Frobenius Jordan blocks.</p>", "proof": "<p>At the moving place \\(Q_v=q_i\\to1\\), binary roots tend to one and the head tends to \\(V(\\gamma)\\); Proposition 2 gives the bound. At fixed potentially good places, head roots have purity size \\(Q_v^{3/2}\\), while final roots are \\(Q_v,Q_v^2\\) up to the specified unitary factor. Half-integral weight difference prevents integral norm-power coincidence. At ramified potentially multiplicative places, choose Frobenius with negative quadratic sign, excluding coincidence with positive final roots. These are fixed nonzero comparisons.</p><p>Nonzero resultants exclude coincidences and norm linkages between head and binary allocations. The segment classification factors them into unlinked representations; any crossing segment would produce a forbidden \\(Q_v^d\\) ratio with \\(|d|\\le4\\). The binary allocation is the entire spherical binary representation, with zero predicted monodromy.</p><p>To control actual monodromy under the domination-only compatibility, use the direct-sum construction of Proposition 10. In each shifted cuspidal copy, a selected root has a length-one segment: a longer segment would cross the separation boundary or contradict binary sphericity. Two selected length-one data in a unitary generic cuspidal factor cannot differ by \\(Q_v\\), since their real exponents lie in an interval of width less than one. Actual monodromy cannot link such roots, different shifted copies, or the separated complement. Thus it vanishes on the binary part.</p><p>The selected generalized Frobenius spaces have trivial finite semisimple inertia and zero monodromy. Coprime monic factorization gives the required primary projection, including Jordan blocks.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.3", "orig": "Lemma 8.8, “The Steinberg control.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>At a split place use the prescribed \\((3,1)\\)-parahoric level.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Inertia acts unipotently and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(\\rho_\\pi(\\iota)-1)\\le1\n\\qquad(\\iota\\in I).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Iwahori invariants make semisimple Weil inertia trivial. Write the local parameter as unramified Steinberg blocks. Standard induction surjects onto the representation, and exactness of compact invariants forces \\((3,1)\\)-parahoric invariants in that induction.</p><p>Finite Weyl sign invariants of a Steinberg factor require its positions to be separated by the induced partial flag. A single-step flag permits at most one block of length two, all others length one. Predicted monodromy therefore has rank at most one and square zero. The compatibility bound gives the same actual bound, and\n\\[\n\\rho_\\pi(\\iota)=\\exp(t_p(\\iota)N).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "Proposition 8.9, “Uniform nilpotent control.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(T_i\\) be the image of \\(T_i^0\\) in its characteristic-zero cusp eigensystems. Use \\(\\Delta\\) from Proposition 11.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is \\(C\\), independent of \\(i\\), weights, and \\(q_i\\), such that\n\\[\n\\Delta^C\\ker(T_i^0\\to T_i)=0.\n\\]\nAfter one fixed decrease of precision, the congruence descends to\n\\[\n\\lambda_i:T_i\\longrightarrow\n(\\mathcal O/p^{M_i}\\mathcal O)[t]/(t^{b_*}),\n\\qquad M_i\\to\\infty.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>On an irreducible local representation, the geometric lemma filters the block Jacquet module by at most \\(4!=24\\) allocation terms. Compact Levi invariants and joint generalized eigenspaces of commuting operators are exact.</p><p>At \\(p\\), the unit summand and the two strict slope gaps of Proposition 11 select one allocation to \\((2,1,1)\\). Final \\(\\operatorname{GL}_1\\) centers act as scalars on its sole surviving grade. At a binary place with \\(\\Delta_v\\ne0\\), determinant and degree-one control determine \\(A_v\\), and separation selects one allocation. Full binary hyperspecial invariants are one-dimensional, even for coincident binary inducing characters. Permutations inside the head lie within the Levi Weyl group and do not create another allocation. Thus the controlled operators have no Jordan extension on the separated locus; good spherical operators are already scalar.</p><p>Where \\(\\Delta\\) has eigenvalue zero, it kills every associated allocation grade and lowers the bounded filtration. Tensoring over the bounded number of control places gives a uniform annihilating power, for example the paper’s coarse \\(24^r\\) bound. The operator identity holds integrally because \\(T_i^0\\) is torsion-free.</p><p>The constant coefficient of \\(\\lambda_i^0(\\Delta)\\) is nonzero with bounded valuation by Proposition 11. Its fixed power has a bounded inverse denominator in \\(k[t]/(t^{b_*})\\). Apply this to the annihilation identity; the kernel maps to zero after only a fixed precision loss. Rename the still-increasing precisions.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "Sections 9.1–9.2, with Lemmas 9.1–9.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Put \\(b=b_*\\), \\(R_b=k[t]/(t^b)\\), and use the reduced orders \\(T_i\\) and maps \\(\\lambda_i\\) of Proposition 12.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\rho_i\\) be the product of the representations of Proposition 10 over all systems of \\(T_i\\), with stable integral lattices, and set\n  \\[\n  B_i=T_i[\\rho_i].\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Form\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal A=\\prod_{\\mathcal U}T_i.\n  \\]\n  The constant-coefficient ultralimit defines \\(\\mathcal A\\to\\mathcal O\\subset k\\); let \\(\\mathfrak q\\) be its kernel. Coefficientwise ultralimits define a map to \\(R_b\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal T=(\\mathcal A_{\\mathfrak q})^h,\n  \\qquad\n  \\lambda:\\mathcal T\\to R_b,\n  \\qquad\n  B=\\left(\\prod_{\\mathcal U}B_i\\right)\\otimes_{\\mathcal A}\\mathcal T.\n  \\]\n  Its residue field is \\(k\\); write \\(\\mathfrak m=\\ker(\\mathcal T\\to k)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal C_{\\mathrm{amb}}\n  =\n  \\prod_{\\mathcal U}\\prod_{\\pi\\text{ at }i}\\overline{\\mathbf Q}_p,\n  \\qquad\n  \\mathcal R=\\mathcal C_{\\mathrm{amb}}\\otimes_{\\mathcal A}\\mathcal T.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Gamma\\) be the ultraproduct of the Galois groups unramified outside \\(Sq_i\\), with the prescribed local subgroups.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The algebra \\(B\\) is finite over \\(\\mathcal T\\), generated by uniformly many group-image monomials, and has a faithful realization in \\(M_4(\\mathcal R)\\), where \\(\\mathcal R\\) is reduced and absolutely flat. Its trace is \\(\\mathcal T\\)-valued and under \\(\\lambda\\) becomes\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}(f\\oplus x\\oplus y),\n\\qquad\nf=V(-2)\\psi^{\\pm1},\\quad\nx=\\epsilon^{-1},\\quad\ny=\\epsilon^{-2}.\n\\]\nThe diagonal representation factors through \\(B\\).</p><p>There are rank-one orthogonal idempotents\n\\[\ne_1,e_2,e_x,e_y,\n\\qquad e_1+e_2+e_x+e_y=1,\n\\]\nwith inverse matrix units identifying \\(e_1,e_2\\) as the \\(f\\)-block. Let \\(D_{uv}\\), \\(u,v\\in\\{f,x,y\\}\\), be the corresponding single-entry corner modules. Then\n\\[\nD_{uu}=\\mathcal T,\n\\qquad\n\\lambda(D_{uv}D_{vu})=0\\quad(u\\ne v).\n\\]\nFinite-value admissibility and corner determinant identities hold as in Lemmas 13.2 and 13.3.</p>", "proof": "<p>Each \\(B_i\\) is finite in a finite product of integral matrix lattices. Chebotarev approximation places traces and characteristic coefficients of every group image in \\(T_i\\). Lemma 13.1 gives uniform finite generation after ultraproduct and base change.</p><p>Elements outside \\(\\mathfrak q\\) have unit image in \\(R_b\\), so the ultralimit map extends to localization and uniquely to henselization. Localizing at the constant-limit kernel inverts all nonzero constants; the residue field is \\(k\\).</p><p>The injections into the ambient matrix products persist under flat localization and henselization. The ambient product is absolutely flat; localization and étale base change preserve absolute flatness and reducedness. Its field tests are algebraically closed residue ultraproducts, with finite étale choices split there.</p><p>The comparison trace follows from good spherical parameters, uniform convergence \\(\\mathcal C^{m_i}\\to1\\), and arbitrarily precise finite-stage Frobenius approximation. It holds on every sequence. The residual constituents are absolutely irreducible and distinct. Their group images span\n\\[\nM_2(k)\\times k\\times k,\n\\]\nhence span its \\(R_b\\)-version by Nakayama. If a relation vanishes in \\(B\\), pairing it with every group-algebra element and using the perfect matrix trace pairing shows its diagonal image vanishes. Thus the diagonal representation factors through \\(B\\).</p><p>Choose an element with four distinct residual eigenvalues. Its characteristic coefficients lie in \\(\\mathcal T\\) by Newton identities; \\(1,2,3,4\\) are units because the residue field has characteristic zero. Henselian splitting gives four simple roots and polynomial rank-one projectors. Lift the two residual \\(f\\)-matrix units and rescale their unit products to make them inverse. Each diagonal corner equals its trace times its idempotent, hence is exactly \\(\\mathcal T\\). Crossing cycles vanish under the diagonal map and therefore under \\(\\lambda\\).</p><p>Lemmas 13.2 and 13.3 provide the asserted admissibility and determinant transport.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.1", "orig": "Lemma 9.1, “Uniformly many module generators.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use \\(T_i,B_i,\\rho_i\\) of Proposition 13.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is \\(N\\), independent of \\(i\\), such that \\(B_i\\) is generated over \\(T_i\\) by \\(N\\) monomials in Galois images.</p>", "proof": "<p>The residual characteristic polynomials are fixed by the comparison system. After one fixed finite extension of \\(L\\) killing their semisimplification, every residual image is unipotent and the integral image pro-\\(p\\). The representations are unramified outside fixed \\(S\\) and a bounded number of places above \\(q_i\\).</p><p>The maximal pro-\\(p\\) Galois group with these ramification conditions has uniformly bounded generator number: its \\(H^1(-,\\mathbf F_p)\\) is bounded by the fixed unit, class-group, and \\(S\\)-contributions plus bounded contributions per new prime. Add fixed finite-extension coset representatives. Uniformly many elements topologically generate the image.</p><p>The algebraic span is closed in the finite integral matrix lattice. Inverses are integral characteristic-polynomial expressions because determinants are units. Hence these elements generate the algebra.</p><p>Modulo the maximal ideal of \\(T_i\\), the degree-eight standard identity for \\(4\\times4\\) matrices holds. Shirshov height bounds the number of power factors needed in spanning words, with base-word length less than eight. Each base word is a group image satisfying its degree-four characteristic polynomial over \\(T_i\\), so reduce its powers to exponents at most three. This leaves a uniformly bounded spanning list modulo the maximal ideal. Nakayama lifts it to \\(B_i\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.2", "orig": "Lemma 9.2, “Finite-value admissibility.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let\n\\[\n\\Phi:B\\longrightarrow M_4(R_b)\n\\]\nbe a \\(\\mathcal T\\)-linear algebra map with scalar map \\(\\lambda\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The matrix coefficients of \\(\\Phi\\rho_*\\) are admissible. The same holds with \\(k\\) in place of \\(R_b\\). Local algebraic identities holding on all local sequences hold uniformly to increasing finite-stage precision.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose the \\(N\\) monomial generators \\(b_{ij}\\) of Lemma 13.1, with images \\(b_j\\in B\\). Only finitely many matrices\n\\[\nC_j=\\Phi(b_j)\n\\]\nare needed, so one denominator clears them.</p><p>For increasing \\(s_i\\), the compact surjection \\(T_i^N\\to B_i\\) gives surjections on finite quotients modulo \\(p^{s_i}\\). Choose sections. Composing with \\(\\rho_i\\) gives locally constant coefficients \\(a_{ij}(g)\\) with\n\\[\n\\rho_i(g)\\equiv\\sum_{j=1}^Na_{ij}(g)b_{ij}\n\\pmod{p^{s_i}B_i}.\n\\]\nApply \\(\\lambda_i\\) and increasingly accurate approximations to the fixed \\(C_j\\). Their limits equal \\(\\Phi\\rho_*\\) by linearity and have the single fixed denominator. Slow precision to the minimum of \\(s_i,M_i\\), minus the fixed loss. The sequence \\((p^{s_i})_i\\) maps to zero coefficientwise under \\(\\lambda\\), even though constant \\(p\\) is invertible in \\(\\mathcal T\\).</p><p>If an identity held on all sequences but failed uniformly, selecting a violating tuple at each index on an ultrafilter-large set would contradict it. The same applies to splittings after fixing their finitely many coefficients. No coordinatewise lifting of henselian idempotents is required.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.3", "orig": "Lemma 9.3, “Corner determinant identities.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the rank-one corners \\(1,2,x,y\\) in Proposition 13.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For four rows of a stack of \\(4\\times4\\) matrices in \\(B\\), with all four column indices, suppose the row indices are the column indices with \\(y\\) replaced by \\(u\\). Their signed determinant defines an element of \\(D_{uy}\\).</p><p>The same holds for a square minor when cancellation of common row and column indices leaves one surplus row and one surplus column; with no surplus it lies in \\(\\mathcal T\\). If the ordinary determinant vanishes in every ambient field, this corner element is zero. The identities survive algebra maps out of \\(B\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Regard each determinant monomial as a directed graph from column to row indices. Incoming and outgoing counts agree except along a path from \\(y\\) to \\(u\\). Decompose into that path and cycles. Compose the path in \\(D_{uy}\\); each cycle is a scalar in a rank-one diagonal corner, hence in \\(\\mathcal T\\). The inverse matrix units identify the two \\(f\\)-positions when needed.</p><p>In a rank-one realization this is the usual product of entries, independent of basis because intermediate changes cancel. Faithfulness in \\(M_4(\\mathcal R)\\) and reducedness of \\(\\mathcal R\\) give the vanishing assertion. Only sums and corner products are used, so algebra maps preserve it.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "Proposition 9.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use \\(\\mathcal T,B,\\mathfrak m\\), the corner modules, and local identities of Propositions 11 and 13.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nD_{xy}=D_{xf}D_{fy}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Put\n\\[\nQ_{xy}\n=\nD_{xy}/(D_{xf}D_{fy}+\\mathfrak mD_{xy}).\n\\]\nA functional \\(Q_{xy}\\to k\\) gives a triangular algebra representation retaining separate \\(f\\) and an extension of \\(y\\) by \\(x\\). Products through \\(f\\) are killed; other crossing products vanish under the cycle relation. It gives an injection\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}_k(Q_{xy},k)\n\\hookrightarrow H_{\\mathrm{adm}}^1(\\Gamma,k(1)).\n\\]\nIndeed, a splitting preserved by the group is preserved by its generated algebra and idempotents, so must be the diagonal \\(y\\)-line and annihilates the functional. Proposition 13 gives admissibility.</p><p>At a conjugate \\(p\\)-place, stack\n\\[\nS_g=\\rho_*(g)-Y_{w^c}(g).\n\\]\nThe characteristic-zero common kernel makes all four-row determinants of row types \\(f,f,x,x\\) zero in \\(D_{xy}\\). They survive the triangular map.</p><p>Choose two transported \\(f\\)-rows and one \\(x\\)-row with invertible complementary \\(3\\times3\\) minor. The \\(f\\)-rows exist because\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}_{G_{L_{w^c}}}(y,f)\n=\nH^0(L_{w^c},V)=0,\n\\]\nand the \\(x\\)-row because \\(x\\ne y\\). Normalize the \\(y\\)-coordinate to one and solve. Every additional \\(x\\)-row is consistent by its determinant identity; \\(f\\)-coordinates are zero and remaining equations hold. This gives a common \\(y\\)-eigenline and local splitting.</p><p>At the distinguished \\(p\\)-place, use the common quotient \\(X_w\\) and transpose the argument. The competing condition is\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}_{G_{L_w}}(f,x)=H^0(L_w,V)=0,\n\\]\nusing \\(V\\simeq V^*(1)\\). Thus the extension is strict at all \\(p\\)-places.</p><p>Away from \\(p\\), it is unramified outside controls. At a binary control, \\(\\Delta_v\\) is a unit in \\(\\mathcal T\\), so the binary projector exists in \\(B\\) and the inertia identity of Proposition 11 survives. It is the identity on the \\(x,y\\)-block, so inertia there is trivial, including at \\(q_i\\).</p><p>At a Steinberg control, the rank-one inertia bound gives vanishing \\(2\\times2\\) minors, which in the triangular quotient read\n\\[\n(f(\\iota)-1)_{ab}u(\\iota)=0.\n\\]\nChoose inertia \\(h\\) with \\(f(h)-1\\ne0\\). This forces \\(u(\\iota)=0\\) whenever \\(f(\\iota)-1\\ne0\\). If \\(f(\\iota)=1\\), apply it to \\(\\iota h\\) and use the cocycle equation to conclude \\(u(\\iota)=0\\).</p><p>Moving unramifiedness and Proposition 2 descend the class to ordinary \\(H^1(L,k(1))\\). Kummer theory identifies classes unramified away from \\(p\\) and zero at \\(p\\) with \\(p\\)-units subject to zero valuations and logarithms. Valuations leave global units. The CM biquadratic \\(L\\) has unit rank one; its fundamental unit has nonzero \\(p\\)-adic logarithm at a split place, since a unit in \\(\\mathbf Q_p^\\times\\) of zero logarithm is a root of unity. Thus the class vanishes.</p><p>Hence \\(Q_{xy}=0\\). Nakayama applied to the finite quotient \\(D_{xy}/D_{xf}D_{fy}\\) proves the identity.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 15", "orig": "Corollary 9.6, with Lemma 9.5", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the limiting algebra of Proposition 13 and the corner identity of Proposition 14.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\operatorname{Fitt}_{0,\\mathcal T}(D_{fy})=0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(a\\in\\mathcal T\\) annihilates \\(D_{fy}\\), Lemma 15.1 makes its image zero in every ambient field. Since \\(\\mathcal T\\) embeds in reduced \\(\\mathcal R\\), \\(a=0\\).</p><p>For a finite module over an arbitrary commutative ring, its zeroth Fitting ideal lies in its annihilator: choose finitely many generators and apply the adjugate identity to square matrices of relations. No finite presentation is required. Hence the Fitting ideal is zero.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.1", "orig": "Lemma 9.5, “Excluding a stable last line.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Retain Proposition 15.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The image of \\(D_{fy}\\) is nonzero at every field evaluation of the ambient ring \\(\\mathcal R\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose \\(D_{fy}\\) vanished in an ambient field. Proposition 14 also kills \\(D_{xy}\\), making the \\(e_y\\)-line globally stable.</p><p>At a conjugate \\(p\\)-place, choose the two \\(f\\)-rows and one \\(x\\)-row from the common-kernel stack, with complementary determinant a unit in \\(\\mathcal T\\). Its residual determinant is invertible in \\(f\\oplus x\\oplus y\\). Once \\(e_y\\) is stable, competing column entries vanish, so the common kernel is exactly \\(e_y\\) and its local character is \\(Y_{w^c}\\).</p><p>These facts descend to arrays of actual systems: stability is checked on the bounded generator list, and rank, matrix-unit, and complementary-minor conditions use finitely many equations. Include the fixed unit element and its conjugate used below. Henselian choices are represented in an algebraically closed residue ultraproduct; finite equations and inequalities hold on an ultrafilter-large set. Thus almost every such system has a one-dimensional constituent \\(\\xi\\) with the prescribed local character.</p><p>The character is de Rham, hence locally algebraic. Global class field theory and the Serre-group construction identify it with an algebraic Hecke character. Its conjugate weight sums are parallel, by evaluating the infinity type on real-subfield units; call the common integer \\(w(\\xi)\\).</p><p>The character lies in a shifted cuspidal factor \\(\\tau[a]\\). If \\(a&gt;1\\), its occurrence in consecutive shifts gives an adjacent integer weight. But its conjugate local weight is \\(3-h_4\\), the lowest weight, separated from the next by the arbitrarily large \\(h_4-h_3\\). Therefore \\(a=1\\).</p><p>At an unramified place, roots of a unitary generic cuspidal local factor have absolute sizes strictly between \\(Q^{-1/2}\\) and \\(Q^{1/2}\\). After the cohomological shift, \\(\\xi\\) has size strictly between \\(Q\\) and \\(Q^2\\). An algebraic Hecke character with conjugate sum \\(w(\\xi)\\) has size \\(Q^{w(\\xi)/2}\\). Consequently\n\\[\nw(\\xi)=3.\n\\]</p><p>The fixed \\(p\\)-level \\(J\\) permits only finitely many inertial cuspidal-support classes, hence finitely many semisimple inertia types. The smooth parts of these rank-one characters therefore vanish on one fixed open subgroup of local units. Choose \\(g\\) there with \\(\\epsilon(g)\\) nontorsion. For its conjugate \\(g^c\\),\n\\[\n\\xi(g)\\xi(g^c)=\\epsilon(g)^{-3}.\n\\]\nThis holds in the ambient evaluation.</p><p>However,\n\\[\n(e_y\\rho_*(g)e_y)(e_y\\rho_*(g^c)e_y)\n-\\epsilon(g)^{-3}e_y\n\\]\nis a unit in \\(\\mathcal Te_y\\): its residue is\n\\[\n(\\epsilon(g)^{-4}-\\epsilon(g)^{-3})e_y\\ne0\n\\]\nbecause residual \\(y=\\epsilon^{-2}\\). A unit cannot vanish in an ambient field. This contradiction proves nonvanishing.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 16", "orig": "Proposition 9.7, “Extension extraction.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use \\(b=b_*\\), \\(R_b=k[t]/(t^b)\\), the limiting corner module \\(D_{fy}\\), and admissible Greenberg cohomology from Proposition 2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an injective \\(R_b\\)-linear map\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}_{R_b}\n(D_{fy}\\otimes_{\\mathcal T,\\lambda}R_b,R_b)\n\\hookrightarrow\nH_{\\mathrm{Gr}}^1(L,V\\otimes\\psi^{\\pm1}).\n\\]\nIts source has \\(R_b\\)-length at least \\(b\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(M=D_{fy}\\otimes_{\\mathcal T,\\lambda}R_b\\). A functional \\(M\\to R_b\\) defines a triangular extension of \\(y\\) by \\(f\\), retaining separate \\(x\\).</p><p>The additional discarded path is\n\\[\nD_{fx}D_{xy}=D_{fx}D_{xf}D_{fy}\n\\]\nby Proposition 14. Its first factors form a crossing cycle killed by \\(\\lambda\\). Other discarded products are killed by the same cycle relation or the diagonal map. Thus the assignment is an algebra map. A split group extension has its splitting preserved by \\(B\\) and its idempotents, forcing the functional to vanish. This proves injectivity. Since\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}(y,f)=V\\otimes\\psi^{\\pm1},\n\\]\nand Proposition 13 gives admissibility, it remains to check the one-sided condition.</p><p>At a conjugate \\(p\\)-place, use\n\\[\nS_g=\\rho_*(g)-Y_{w^c}(g).\n\\]\nVanishing determinants of row types \\(f,f,x,f\\) give corner identities in \\(D_{fy}\\). Two \\(f\\)-rows and one \\(x\\)-row have a complementary minor with invertible residue, hence a unit over \\(R_b\\), by\n\\[\nH^0(L_{w^c},V)=0,\\qquad x\\ne y.\n\\]\nSolve with \\(y\\)-coordinate one. Varying the last \\(f\\)-row proves all equations, with zero \\(x\\)-coordinate and vanishing \\(y\\)-rows. This supplies a common \\(y\\)-eigenline and splits the local extension over the entire Artin ring.</p><p>The condition is full at \\(\\Sigma_p\\) and relaxed at remaining fixed places. Away from the fixed and moving places, the representations are unramified. These are precisely the admissible Greenberg conditions.</p><p>By Proposition 15 and base change,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Fitt}_{0,R_b}(M)=0.\n\\]\nWrite its elementary-divisor decomposition\n\\[\nM\\simeq\\bigoplus_jR_b/(t^{a_j}),\n\\qquad1\\le a_j\\le b.\n\\]\nIts zeroth Fitting ideal is \\((t^{\\sum_ja_j})\\), with free summands interpreted by \\(a_j=b\\). Vanishing means \\(\\sum_ja_j\\ge b\\). Moreover\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}_{R_b}(R_b/(t^{a_j}),R_b)=(t^{b-a_j})\n\\]\nhas length \\(a_j\\). The dual source therefore has length at least \\(b\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 17", "orig": "the full-corank descent bound in the proof of Corollary 10.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\ell\\equiv4,7,8\\pmod9\\) be prime.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the isomorphic Weierstrass model and companion\n  \\[\n  E_\\ell:\\ y^2=x^3-27(4\\ell)^2,\n  \\qquad\n  E_\\ell':\\ y^2=x^3+(4\\ell)^2.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  \\lambda:E_\\ell\\to E_\\ell',\n  \\qquad\n  \\lambda':E_\\ell'\\to E_\\ell,\n  \\qquad\n  \\lambda'\\lambda=[3].\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S,S'\\) be the two isogeny Selmer groups with local Kummer conditions, and put\n  \\[\n  a=\\dim_{\\mathbf F_3}\\Sha(E_\\ell/\\mathbf Q)[\\lambda],\n  \\qquad\n  b=\\dim_{\\mathbf F_3}\\Sha(E_\\ell'/\\mathbf Q)[\\lambda'].\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use Satgé’s paired descents, retaining their Tate–Shafarevich terms.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\ns_3(E_\\ell)\\le1.\n\\]\nNo finiteness of \\(\\Sha(E_\\ell/\\mathbf Q)\\) is assumed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Satgé’s descent sequences make \\(a,b\\) finite. His Proposition 1.2 gives\n\\[\n\\dim_{\\mathbf F_3}S=\\dim_{\\mathbf F_3}S'=1.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\ell&gt;3\\), his equation (2) gives the exact identity\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}E_\\ell(\\mathbf Q)=1-a-b.\n\\]</p><p>Functoriality of localization gives isogeny maps on \\(\\Sha\\), with composite \\([3]\\). Thus \\(\\lambda_*\\) maps\n\\[\n\\Sha(E_\\ell/\\mathbf Q)[3]\n\\longrightarrow\n\\Sha(E_\\ell'/\\mathbf Q)[\\lambda']\n\\]\nwith kernel \\(\\Sha(E_\\ell/\\mathbf Q)[\\lambda]\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\dim_{\\mathbf F_3}\\Sha(E_\\ell/\\mathbf Q)[3]\\le a+b.\n\\]</p><p>The full Kummer sequence gives\n\\[\ns_3(E_\\ell)\n=\n\\operatorname{rank}E_\\ell(\\mathbf Q)\n+\n\\operatorname{corank}_{\\mathbf Z_3}\\Sha(E_\\ell/\\mathbf Q)[3^\\infty].\n\\]\nThe latter group is cofinitely generated, and its Pontryagin-dual structure theorem gives corank at most its \\(3\\)-torsion dimension. Therefore\n\\[\ns_3(E_\\ell)\n\\le(1-a-b)+(a+b)=1.\n\\]</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [4, 0, "cite"], [4, 6, "cite"], [4, 7, "cite"], [5, 4, "cite"], [6, 0, "cite"], [6, 12, "cite"], [6, 17, "cite"], [6, 42, "cite"], [6, 49, "cite"], [6, 52, "cite"], [7, 6, "cite"], [8, 6, "cite"], [8, 11, "cite"], [9, 6, "cite"], [9, 10, "cite"], [10, 6, "cite"], [10, 11, "cite"], [11, 6, "cite"], [12, 0, "cite"], [12, 24, "cite"], [13, 12, "cite"], [14, 0, "cite"], [14, 21, "cite"], [14, 33, "cite"], [15, 14, "cite"], [16, 14, "cite"], [17, 0, "cite"], [17, 22, "cite"], [17, 27, "cite"], [17, 30, "cite"], [18, 17, "cite"], [19, 17, "cite"], [20, 21, "cite"], [21, 17, "cite"], [22, 0, "cite"], [22, 27, "cite"], [22, 30, "cite"], [23, 22, "cite"], [23, 25, "cite"], [23, 26, "cite"], [24, 22, "cite"], [25, 22, "cite"], [26, 22, "cite"], [27, 0, "cite"], [27, 32, "cite"], [27, 33, "cite"], [28, 30, "cite"], [29, 30, "cite"], [30, 27, "cite"], [31, 27, "cite"], [32, 0, "cite"], [32, 36, "cite"], [32, 39, "cite"], [32, 40, "cite"], [33, 32, "cite"], [34, 32, "cite"], [35, 32, "cite"], [36, 0, "cite"], [36, 39, "cite"], [37, 36, "cite"], [37, 38, "cite"], [38, 36, "cite"], [39, 0, "cite"], [39, 40, "cite"], [39, 41, "cite"], [39, 42, "cite"], [39, 45, "cite"], [40, 0, "cite"], [40, 44, "cite"], [40, 49, "cite"], [41, 40, "cite"], [42, 40, "cite"], [43, 40, "cite"], [44, 0, "cite"], [44, 45, "cite"], [45, 0, "cite"], [45, 49, "cite"], [45, 50, "cite"], [45, 52, "cite"], [46, 45, "cite"], [46, 47, "cite"], [47, 45, "cite"], [48, 45, "cite"], [49, 0, "cite"], [49, 50, "cite"], [49, 51, "cite"], [49, 52, "cite"], [50, 0, "cite"], [50, 52, "cite"], [51, 50, "cite"], [52, 0, "cite"], [53, 1, "cite"]]});
